{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PAfHgyBPWaLwnLhRdj4hWw","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL 2024 Review Form Structure\n\nBased on the provided content, **the specific review form structure, numeric scoring scale, and detailed evaluation rubric are not included** in this document.\n\nThe page outlines review criteria and desk rejection guidelines, but does not contain:\n- A numbered scoring scale (e.g., 1-10)\n- Specific evaluation dimensions with point values\n- Detailed rubric definitions for each score level\n\n## What IS Provided:\n\nThe document specifies that submissions will be evaluated on:\n- \"Significance and novelty of the results, either theoretical or empirical\"\n- \"Degree to which they have been objectively established\"\n- \"Potential for scientific and technological impact\"\n- \"Relevance to robotic learning\"\n\nAdditionally, papers must satisfy scope requirements by demonstrating relevance through either explicit intent to address \"a learning question for physical robots\" OR outcomes showing \"the proposed learning solution on physical robots.\"\n\n**Recommendation:** The actual review form with numeric scales and detailed scoring rubrics likely appears in a separate document or the review submission system itself, not in these instruction guidelines."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"937facd1-3ff3-4809-b45a-58cd38966ef5","uuid":"a16597ca-9bf9-48a7-9cc3-832348b3454f","timestamp":"2026-05-20T17:07:07.203Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":133912,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL 2024 Review Form Structure\n\nBased on the provided content, **the specific review form structure, numeric scoring scale, and detailed evaluation rubric are not included** in this document.\n\nThe page outlines review criteria and desk rejection guidelines, but does not contain:\n- A numbered scoring scale (e.g., 1-10)\n- Specific evaluation dimensions with point values\n- Detailed rubric definitions for each score level\n\n## What IS Provided:\n\nThe document specifies that submissions will be evaluated on:\n- \"Significance and novelty of the results, either theoretical or empirical\"\n- \"Degree to which they have been objectively established\"\n- \"Potential for scientific and technological impact\"\n- \"Relevance to robotic learning\"\n\nAdditionally, papers must satisfy scope requirements by demonstrating relevance through either explicit intent to address \"a learning question for physical robots\" OR outcomes showing \"the proposed learning solution on physical robots.\"\n\n**Recommendation:** The actual review form with numeric scales and detailed scoring rubrics likely appears in a separate document or the review submission system itself, not in these instruction guidelines.","durationMs":4016,"url":"https://2024.corl.org/contributions/instruction-for-reviews"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RP1vxAvDEgAfeiMqae1f4C","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL 2023 Review Form Structure\n\nBased on the provided guidelines, I cannot extract a complete numeric scoring scale or detailed rubric, as **the document does not specify exact score numbers or point values**.\n\n## Evaluation Dimensions\n\nThe guidelines identify six core evaluation criteria:\n\n1. **Originality** — novelty of problems/methods and differentiation from prior work\n2. **Quality** — technical soundness and empirical/theoretical support\n3. **Clarity** — writing quality and reproducibility information\n4. **Significance** — importance and potential impact on the field\n5. **Relevance** — alignment with robot learning and CoRL audience interests\n6. **Limitations** — honest reporting of constraints (treated favorably)\n\n## Additional Requirements\n\nThe document mandates that CoRL papers \"include a limitations section\" and emphasizes treating honestly-reported limitations \"kindly.\" However, **unreported obvious shortcomings should be strongly reflected in scores**.\n\n## Missing Information\n\nThe provided content does not include:\n- Numeric scoring scale (e.g., 1-10, 1-5)\n- Score interpretation (what each number means)\n- Acceptance thresholds\n- Detailed rubric mappings\n\nThe guidelines reference ICML and NeurIPS resources for additional reviewer guidance but do not reproduce their specific scoring frameworks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"937facd1-3ff3-4809-b45a-58cd38966ef5","uuid":"d1143243-4dfa-45a0-a97a-81e68789553d","timestamp":"2026-05-20T17:07:09.322Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":105095,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL 2023 Review Form Structure\n\nBased on the provided guidelines, I cannot extract a complete numeric scoring scale or detailed rubric, as **the document does not specify exact score numbers or point values**.\n\n## Evaluation Dimensions\n\nThe guidelines identify six core evaluation criteria:\n\n1. **Originality** — novelty of problems/methods and differentiation from prior work\n2. **Quality** — technical soundness and empirical/theoretical support\n3. **Clarity** — writing quality and reproducibility information\n4. **Significance** — importance and potential impact on the field\n5. **Relevance** — alignment with robot learning and CoRL audience interests\n6. **Limitations** — honest reporting of constraints (treated favorably)\n\n## Additional Requirements\n\nThe document mandates that CoRL papers \"include a limitations section\" and emphasizes treating honestly-reported limitations \"kindly.\" However, **unreported obvious shortcomings should be strongly reflected in scores**.\n\n## Missing Information\n\nThe provided content does not include:\n- Numeric scoring scale (e.g., 1-10, 1-5)\n- Score interpretation (what each number means)\n- Acceptance thresholds\n- Detailed rubric mappings\n\nThe guidelines reference ICML and NeurIPS resources for additional reviewer guidance but do not reproduce their specific scoring frameworks.","durationMs":5360,"url":"https://www.corl2023.org/reviewer-guidelines"}}
