{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QXAFhHxi4Gutm7YnojBgBQ","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL 2021 Review Form Structure\n\nBased on the provided content, **the review form structure, scoring scale, evaluation dimensions, and acceptance thresholds are not detailed in this document.**\n\nThe page titled \"Instructions for Reviewers\" focuses exclusively on procedural information: the review timeline, reviewer responsibilities across three phases, and general guidance about the review process. It emphasizes that reviewers should \"provide constructive feedback to the authors\" and maintain \"active and constructive engagement\" during discussions, but it does not specify:\n\n- The numerical scoring scale or what each score represents\n- Specific evaluation criteria or dimensions\n- Quantitative acceptance thresholds\n- The actual review form template\n\nTo locate this information, reviewers would need to consult additional resources, likely available through OpenReview or in separate reviewer guidelines not included in this excerpt."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"937facd1-3ff3-4809-b45a-58cd38966ef5","uuid":"f898c8b7-29f4-482c-a60e-cb67d97e5322","timestamp":"2026-05-20T17:07:37.995Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":125042,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL 2021 Review Form Structure\n\nBased on the provided content, **the review form structure, scoring scale, evaluation dimensions, and acceptance thresholds are not detailed in this document.**\n\nThe page titled \"Instructions for Reviewers\" focuses exclusively on procedural information: the review timeline, reviewer responsibilities across three phases, and general guidance about the review process. It emphasizes that reviewers should \"provide constructive feedback to the authors\" and maintain \"active and constructive engagement\" during discussions, but it does not specify:\n\n- The numerical scoring scale or what each score represents\n- Specific evaluation criteria or dimensions\n- Quantitative acceptance thresholds\n- The actual review form template\n\nTo locate this information, reviewers would need to consult additional resources, likely available through OpenReview or in separate reviewer guidelines not included in this excerpt.","durationMs":3707,"url":"https://2021.corl.org/reviewer-information/instructions-for-reviewers"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KLZSsmcXTnq7HnoDLV2SRW","type":"tool_result","content":"# CoRL 2023 Rating System\n\nBased on the Paper Copilot statistics page, here's what I found about CoRL's scoring system:\n\n**Scoring Scale:**\nThe data shows ratings ranging from approximately 4.2 to 10.0, indicating \"a 10-point scale\" for reviewer evaluations.\n\n**CoRL 2023 Acceptance Rate:**\nThe conference accepted 199 papers out of 498 total submissions, resulting in a 39.96% acceptance rate. This breaks down as:\n- Posters: 33.33% (166 papers)\n- Oral presentations: 6.63% (33 papers)\n\n**Score Distribution:**\nThe statistics reveal the following characteristics:\n- Average rating: 6.52 (standard deviation: 1.02)\n- Accepted papers averaged 6.52 (range: 4.70-10.00)\n- Poster acceptances averaged 6.27\n- Oral acceptances averaged 7.73\n\nThe data suggests that papers receiving higher scores (particularly 7.73+ average) are more likely to receive oral presentation slots, while those scoring around 6.27 typically receive poster assignments."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"937facd1-3ff3-4809-b45a-58cd38966ef5","uuid":"a4a13b2d-107f-4cff-acaf-ec484cad81bc","timestamp":"2026-05-20T17:07:38.188Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":223077,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CoRL 2023 Rating System\n\nBased on the Paper Copilot statistics page, here's what I found about CoRL's scoring system:\n\n**Scoring Scale:**\nThe data shows ratings ranging from approximately 4.2 to 10.0, indicating \"a 10-point scale\" for reviewer evaluations.\n\n**CoRL 2023 Acceptance Rate:**\nThe conference accepted 199 papers out of 498 total submissions, resulting in a 39.96% acceptance rate. This breaks down as:\n- Posters: 33.33% (166 papers)\n- Oral presentations: 6.63% (33 papers)\n\n**Score Distribution:**\nThe statistics reveal the following characteristics:\n- Average rating: 6.52 (standard deviation: 1.02)\n- Accepted papers averaged 6.52 (range: 4.70-10.00)\n- Poster acceptances averaged 6.27\n- Oral acceptances averaged 7.73\n\nThe data suggests that papers receiving higher scores (particularly 7.73+ average) are more likely to receive oral presentation slots, while those scoring around 6.27 typically receive poster assignments.","durationMs":4792,"url":"https://papercopilot.com/statistics/corl-statistics/corl-2023-statistics/"}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GwybEDDkcQwFEhnxhRyYSR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have enough information about the CoRL scoring system:\n\n- CoRL uses a 1-10 scale (data shows scores from ~4.2 to 10.0)\n- CoRL 2023 acceptance rate: ~40%\n- Average accepted paper rating: ~6.52\n- Oral average: ~7.73\n- Poster average: ~6.27\n- Evaluation dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance, Limitations\n\nNow I have all the information needed. Let me proceed with the dream review.\n\nLet me now structure the review process:\n\n1. I need to dispatch 2 reviewers: R1 (methods + experiments) and R2 (novelty + related work)\n\n\n2. Run a full roundtable with independent reviews, cross-sharing, convergence, and synthesis\n3. Submit the review report\n\nLet me now carefully analyze the paper and context YAML, then dispatch the two reviewers as agents. The paper is a 12-page diagnostic study on conditioning-induced value overestimation in DreamerV3, examining 31 configurations across four robotic control environments.\n\nThe core finding is that trained dt_emb triggers CIVO, with normalized value error above 1.5x serving as a reliable diagnostic marker (AUROC=0.969). Notably, all attempted interventions—TCN, SGS, RAVE, and uncertainty truncation—failed to resolve the issue, positioning this as a pure diagnostic contribution rather than a solution. The paper makes eight claims, with the eighth moved to the appendix.\n\nThe review context indicates this is a \"worst\" tier prediction with some data being simulated rather than empirical. Cartpole and Hopper results are predicted, TCN step-1 data is partially predicted, and certain seed runs are synthetic. The narrative centers on the failure of all interventions and the diagnostic value of the NVE metric.\n\nNow I'm setting up the parallel reviewer dispatch using the CoRL scoring rubric with dimensions for originality and quality on a 1-10 scale.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":209,"cache_creation_input_tokens":649,"cache_read_input_tokens":71628,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":649},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"937facd1-3ff3-4809-b45a-58cd38966ef5","uuid":"e2beeb6e-97bf-47be-b706-dc1526e600e4"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GwybEDDkcQwFEhnxhRyYSR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have the paper fully read, the experiment context analyzed, and the CoRL scoring rubric established. CoRL uses a 1–10 scale (accepted paper average ~6.5, oral ~7.7, acceptance rate ~40%), evaluating Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, and Relevance.\n\nNow dispatching R1 (methods + experiments) and R2 (novelty + related work) as independent blind reviewers."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":209,"cache_creation_input_tokens":649,"cache_read_input_tokens":71628,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":649},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"937facd1-3ff3-4809-b45a-58cd38966ef5","uuid":"b26810a3-e141-45f8-baed-99df0a4213d7"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GwybEDDkcQwFEhnxhRyYSR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XEbpCEHgN2TEZ2rDrQ5KQK","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **methods** and **experiments**. You must perform a thorough, independent review.\n\n## CoRL Scoring Rubric\n- Scale: 1–10 (1=strong reject, 10=strong accept)\n- Accepted paper average: ~6.5; Oral average: ~7.7; Acceptance rate ~40%\n- Dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance\n- CoRL papers must include a limitations section. Honestly reported limitations should be treated kindly.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation in Variable-Rate World-Model Imagination\"\n\n**Core contribution:** A systematic diagnostic analysis of variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3, identifying a failure mode called Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation (CIVO). When a trained temporal embedding (dt_emb) is used in imagination-based actor-critic, the RSSM's imprecise multi-step predictions create a positive feedback loop of value overestimation and policy degradation. The paper introduces Normalized Value Excess (NVE) as a diagnostic metric (AUROC=0.969 at 1.5× threshold across 18 seeds). All tested interventions fail to robustly eliminate CIVO. The contrast with TAWM (MPC-based, where dt-conditioning succeeds) localizes the vulnerability to the imagination-based actor-critic architecture.\n\n**Claims (C1-C8):**\n- C1: Variable-rate (Δt>1) imagination consistently degrades performance — CONFIRMED, Strong\n- C2: Trained dt_emb reliably triggers pathological value overestimation — CONFIRMED (AUROC=0.969), Strong\n- C3: Untrained dt_emb prevents optimistic drift — CONFIRMED (6/6 seeds), Strong\n- C4: Longer Δt amplifies degradation — CONFIRMED, Moderate\n- C5: Horizon and subsampling are secondary factors — CONFIRMED, Moderate\n- C6: Dual vulnerability pathways (CIVO vs non-CIVO policy instability) — CONFIRMED (4 seeds), Strong\n- C7: Gradient magnitude ≠ causal importance — CONFIRMED (17 seeds), Strong\n- C8: TCN/RAVE mitigation — DROPPED to appendix as negative result\n\n## Paper Structure (12 pages)\n- **Section 1 (Introduction):** Motivates variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3, states that all Δt>1 configs degrade. Defines the CIVO causal chain (4 steps). Lists 4 contributions.\n- **Section 2 (Background/Related Work):** Temporal abstraction in RL (options framework, FiGAR), World-model RL (DreamerV3, IRIS, TWM, THICK, Hieros, TAWM), Value overestimation in MBRL (delusional bias).\n- **Section 3 (Methodology):**\n  - 3.1 Base Architecture: DreamerV3 with RSSM, symlog critic, H=15\n  - 3.2 Variable-Rate Imagination: temporal embedding (dt_emb), temporal subsampling, learned gating\n  - 3.3 Factorial Experiment Design: 2×2×2 grid over subsample/horizon/dt_emb_training (Table 1)\n  - 3.4 Diagnostic Metrics: NVE defined as r_ve^exp(t)/r_ve^baseline(t), threshold NVE>1.5× at t≥35K\n- **Section 4 (Experiments):**\n  - 4.1 C1: Performance degradation across configs/envs (Table 2)\n  - 4.2 C2: Trained dt_emb triggers CIVO. Walker 008d 5 seeds (4/5 collapse, all NVE>1.5×). Causal control: 008f (no dt_emb) 5 seeds all NVE<1.5×. Cross-env: Cheetah 3/3 collapse (NVE 8.3-70.6×), Cartpole 2/3 collapse, Hopper 2/3. NVE threshold analysis (AUROC=0.969, Table 11)\n  - 4.3 C3: Untrained dt_emb. 6 seeds across Walker+Cheetah, all NVE<1.0×, mean 0.54\n  - 4.4 C4: Δt=2 vs Δt=4 monotonic degradation (Table 13)\n  - 4.5 C5: Horizon/subsampling secondary\n  - 4.6 C6: Dual pathways. 008e_v3 (Δt=1 imagination, trained dt_emb): 2/4 collapse (50%), matching 008d. Pathway A = classic CIVO (entropy ↓), Pathway B = policy instability (entropy ↑). Table 15 compares.\n  - 4.7 C7: SGS fails. 3 variants across 17 seeds. Gradient decomposition: reward head 38-49%, obs decoder 16-22%. Frozen dt_emb ablation (NVE=0.42 but gradient starvation). Table 18, 19.\n  - 4.8 RSSM prediction quality. Reward gap 2.78× for trained dt_emb vs 1.56× baseline. Value prediction distributions (65-80% V(s)>G_MC). dt_emb cosine similarity (0.85→0.12). Tables 19, 20.\n- **Section 5 (Discussion):** TAWM contrast (MPC breaks feedback loop), multiple vulnerability pathways, gradient ≠ causality, stochastic bifurcation, implications for temporal abstraction\n- **Section 6 (Conclusion):** Summary\n- **Appendix A:** Comprehensive negative results catalog (TCN dose-response, uncertainty-gated truncation, consolidated intervention summary Table 25, claim status Table 26)\n\n## Experiment Design Reference\nThe full experiment design includes these experiments (some with actual data, some with predicted data):\n\n**Actual data experiments:**\n- Walker Walk 008d (trained dt_emb): 5 seeds, 4/5 collapse, all NVE>1.5×\n- Walker Walk 008f (no dt_emb): s0 (actual), s1 (predicted), s2 (actual), s3, s4 — all NVE<1.5×\n- Walker Walk 008e_v3 (Δt=1 imag): 4 seeds (s1,s2 actual; s3,s4 actual) — 2/4 collapse\n- Walker Walk 008a (untrained dt_emb): 3 seeds actual, all NVE<1.0×\n- Walker Walk frozen dt_emb: 1 seed actual\n- Cheetah baseline: 5 seeds actual\n- Cheetah trained dt_emb: s0 actual (NVE=70.64), s1 actual (NVE=8.27), s2 predicted\n- Cheetah no dt_emb: s0 actual, s1 actual, s2 predicted\n- Cheetah untrained dt_emb: s0 actual, s1 actual, s2 predicted\n- RSSM fidelity analysis: actual\n- Actor entropy analysis: predicted (from existing logs)\n- NVE threshold sensitivity: predicted from existing metrics\n- TCN σ=0.2 Walker s0: actual (successful); s1: partial actual to 85K + predicted beyond\n- TCN σ=0.5 Cheetah s0: partial actual (16K) + predicted\n- TCN σ=0.1 Walker s0: predicted (just started)\n- TCN σ=0.2 PickCube s0: predicted\n\n**Fully predicted experiments (no actual data):**\n- Cartpole Swingup baseline (3 seeds): predicted\n- Cartpole Swingup trained dt_emb (3 seeds): predicted\n- Cartpole Swingup no dt_emb (3 seeds): predicted\n- Hopper Hop validation (3×3 configs): predicted\n- V(s) vs MC return distribution: predicted\n- dt_emb cosine similarity: predicted\n- Uncertainty-gated imagination truncation: predicted\n- TCN σ=0.1 Walker: predicted\n- TCN σ=0.2 PickCube: predicted\n\n## Your Review Task (R1: Methods + Experiments)\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences): Re-express the paper's main contribution\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted): Focus on experimental methodology, factorial design, metric design, causal reasoning, reproducibility\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted): Focus on experimental gaps, statistical rigor, missing controls, questionable methodological choices, environments tested, seed counts\n4. **Questions for authors** (bulleted)\n5. **Missing references or comparisons**\n6. **Scores** (use CoRL 1-10 scale):\n   - Soundness (technical correctness of claims and methodology)\n   - Presentation (clarity, writing quality, figure/table quality)\n   - Contribution (significance of results to the field)\n   - Overall recommendation (1-10 with justification)\n   - Confidence (1-5, where 5=expert)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Evaluate whether the experimental evidence actually supports the claims made. Pay attention to:\n- How many data points are \"predicted\" vs actual\n- Statistical significance with small seed counts\n- Whether the causal claims are justified by the experimental design\n- Whether the environments are diverse enough\n- Whether the NVE metric is well-motivated and the threshold well-justified\n- Quality of the negative results documentation\n\nFormat your review as a structured markdown document."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":209,"cache_creation_input_tokens":649,"cache_read_input_tokens":71628,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":649},"output_tokens":8,"s