{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Dx1cDS7VqMcxfhgEeCuGDB","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Discussion}\n2\t\\label{sec:discussion}\n3\t\n4\tFull mechanistic analysis, gradient decomposition, and seed-divergence discussion are in Appendix~\\ref{sec:appendix_analysis}.\n5\t\n6\t\\paragraph{CIVO mechanism and gradient analysis.}\n7\tThe RSSM reward head develops a $2.78\\times$ prediction gap between $\\dt{=}1$ and $\\dt{=}2$ under trained conditioning (Table~\\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}); the critic internalizes this bias and the actor exploits it, creating a four-stage feedback loop analogous to delusional bias \\citep{lu2018nondelusional} but model-induced. Gradient decomposition (Figure~\\ref{fig:gradient_decomp}) reveals that gradient magnitude does not predict causal importance: the reward head contributes 38--49\\% of $\\nabla_\\theta e_{\\dt}$ yet reward-only SGS fails catastrophically (NVE $10.42\\times$), while the observation decoder (16--22\\%) carries the critical $\\dt$-specific spatial predictions. All three SGS variants exhibit compensatory amplification---blocking any pathway shifts co-adaptation to the remaining ones---and full-pathway SGS (0/3 elimination) establishes forward-pass conditioning as the irreducible vulnerability floor. Additionally, obs-decoder SGS trades overestimation for systematic underestimation: all four Walker seeds reach terminal $v/e$ ratios of $0.056$--$0.14$ by 215K, likely because detaching observation decoder gradients damages the shared RSSM latent space (Appendix~\\ref{sec:seed_divergence}).\n8\t\n9\t\\paragraph{MPC vs.\\ imagination-based actor-critic.}\n10\tTAWM \\citep{nhu2025tawm} succeeds with $\\dt$-conditioning in TD-MPC2 \\citep{hansen2024tdmpc2} because MPC re-plans from real states at every step, so model errors do not accumulate into a critic. In DreamerV3, the actor-critic trains entirely within imagination: the RSSM's fidelity gap at $\\dt > 1$ produces biased value estimates that the actor exploits, closing a positive feedback loop with no MPC counterpart (Appendix~\\ref{sec:mpc_immune}).\n11\t\n12\t\\paragraph{Practitioner guidelines.}\n13\tThe intervention hierarchy (Table~\\ref{tab:intervention_hierarchy}) yields actionable recommendations: (1)~monitor for CIVO using the NVE diagnostic ($\\geq 1.5\\times$, AUROC${=}0.971$, 95\\% CI: 0.904--1.000, $n{=}29$; Appendix~\\ref{sec:roc_analysis}); the Youden-optimal threshold $\\theta^* = 1.53$ coincides with the preset $1.5\\times$ cutoff, confirming this choice is statistically principled; (2)~no tested intervention reliably eliminates CIVO across environments---TCN ($\\sigma{=}0.2$) prevents CIVO on Walker (2/2 seeds) but fails on Cheetah and PickCube, and even successful prevention exhibits high eval variance (45--91\\%); (3)~avoid gradient-level interventions (compensatory amplification) and output-level value capping (anchor pollution).\n14\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XEbpCEHgN2TEZ2rDrQ5KQK","session_id":"937facd1-3ff3-4809-b45a-58cd38966ef5","uuid":"572a5c54-33fe-4665-8cf0-bf4938c71001","timestamp":"2026-05-20T17:09:13.653Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01GwybEDDkcQwFEhnxhRyYSR","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01XfSWuKYrSAZbSpnCBCAjSh","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **novelty** and **related work**. You must perform a thorough, independent review.\n\n## CoRL Scoring Rubric\n- Scale: 1–10 (1=strong reject, 10=strong accept)\n- Accepted paper average: ~6.5; Oral average: ~7.7; Acceptance rate ~40%\n- Dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance\n- CoRL papers must include a limitations section. Honestly reported limitations should be treated kindly.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation in Variable-Rate World-Model Imagination\"\n\n**Core contribution:** A systematic diagnostic analysis of variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3, identifying a failure mode called Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation (CIVO). When a trained temporal embedding (dt_emb) is used in imagination-based actor-critic, the RSSM's imprecise multi-step predictions create a positive feedback loop of value overestimation and policy degradation. The paper introduces Normalized Value Excess (NVE) as a diagnostic metric (AUROC=0.969 at 1.5× threshold across 18 seeds). All tested interventions fail to robustly eliminate CIVO. The contrast with TAWM (MPC-based, where dt-conditioning succeeds) localizes the vulnerability to the imagination-based actor-critic architecture.\n\n**Claims (C1-C8):**\n- C1: Variable-rate imagination consistently degrades — CONFIRMED, Strong\n- C2: Trained dt_emb → CIVO — CONFIRMED (AUROC=0.969), Strong\n- C3: Untrained dt_emb protection — CONFIRMED (6/6 seeds), Strong\n- C4: Longer Δt amplifies — CONFIRMED, Moderate\n- C5: Horizon/subsampling secondary — CONFIRMED, Moderate\n- C6: Dual vulnerability pathways — CONFIRMED (4 seeds), Strong\n- C7: Gradient ≠ causality — CONFIRMED (17 seeds), Strong\n- C8: TCN/RAVE mitigation — DROPPED (appendix negative result)\n\n**Paper Structure (12 pages):**\n- Sec 1 (Intro): Motivates variable-rate imagination, defines CIVO causal chain, 4 contributions\n- Sec 2 (Background/Related Work): \n  - Temporal abstraction in RL: options framework [Sutton et al., 1999], option-critic [Bacon et al., 2017], FiGAR [Sharma et al., 2017], dynamic action repetition\n  - World-model RL: DreamerV3 [Hafner et al., 2023], IRIS [Micheli et al., 2023], TWM [Robine et al., 2023], THICK [Kim et al., 2024], Hieros [Röpke et al., 2024], TAWM [Nhu et al., 2025]\n  - Value overestimation in MBRL: model exploitation [Janner et al., 2019], delusional bias [Lu et al., 2018], ensemble disagreement, pessimistic value estimation\n- Sec 3 (Methodology): DreamerV3 base, variable-rate extensions (dt_emb, subsampling, learned gating), factorial design, NVE metric\n- Sec 4 (Experiments): C1-C7 with tables, cross-environment results (Walker, Cheetah, Cartpole, Hopper), RSSM fidelity analysis, gradient decomposition\n- Sec 5 (Discussion): TAWM contrast, multiple pathways, gradient ≠ causality, stochastic bifurcation, implications\n- Sec 6 (Conclusion)\n- Appendix A: Comprehensive negative results (TCN, uncertainty truncation, consolidated intervention table)\n\n**References cited:** Sutton 1999, Bacon 2017, Sharma 2017, Hafner 2020/2021/2023, Micheli 2023, Robine 2023, Kim 2024, Röpke 2024, Nhu 2025, Janner 2019, Lu 2018 (12 total)\n\n**Key novelty claims:**\n1. CIVO as a new identified failure mode specific to imagination-based AC with temporal conditioning\n2. NVE as a diagnostic metric (AUROC=0.969)\n3. TAWM architectural contrast (MPC succeeds where AC fails) — localizes vulnerability\n4. Gradient magnitude ≠ causal importance (methodological insight from C7)\n5. Dual vulnerability pathways (Pathway A: value-driven, Pathway B: eval-driven)\n6. Comprehensive negative results catalog (12 intervention strategies documented)\n\n**Environments:** Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup, Hopper Hop (all DeepMind Control Suite)\n\n## Your Review Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences): Re-express the paper's main contribution\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted): Focus on novelty of the CIVO finding, positioning against prior work, TAWM contrast insight, NVE metric contribution, thoroughness of negative results\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted): Focus on:\n   - Missing related work (are there other papers on value overestimation in world models, temporal abstraction failures, dt-conditioning issues?)\n   - Novelty concerns (how different is CIVO from known model exploitation/delusional bias? Is the causal chain truly new?)\n   - Scope of environments (all DMC — is this sufficient for CoRL which emphasizes real robots?)\n   - Whether the paper's \"pure diagnostic\" framing is a strength or weakness for CoRL\n   - Whether 12 references is sufficient\n   - Incremental vs significant contribution assessment\n4. **Questions for authors** (bulleted)\n5. **Missing references or comparisons** — specifically look for:\n   - Other works on value overestimation in latent-dynamics models\n   - Papers on temporal abstraction in model-based RL beyond those cited\n   - Works on model exploitation mitigation (MBPO, etc.)\n   - Any papers that have already studied dt-conditioning failures\n   - Relevant robotics applications of variable-rate planning\n6. **Scores** (use CoRL 1-10 scale):\n   - Soundness\n   - Presentation\n   - Contribution\n   - Overall recommendation (1-10 with justification)\n   - Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. A diagnostic paper that identifies a fundamental failure mode can be valuable even without a solution — but assess whether the novelty is sufficient for a top venue. Consider whether this paper is more suited to CoRL (robotics focus) or a general ML venue (ICML/NeurIPS).\n\nFormat your review as a structured markdown document."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":209,"cache_creation_input_tokens":649,"cache_read_input_tokens":71628,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":649},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"937facd1-3ff3-4809-b45a-58cd38966ef5","uuid":"df192978-8256-41e7-9c31-383382a18d31"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"aca5b7fc21eb293c7","tool_use_id":"toolu_01XfSWuKYrSAZbSpnCBCAjSh","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for a CoRL 2026 submission. Your focus areas are **novelty** and **related work**. You must perform a thorough, independent review.\n\n## CoRL Scoring Rubric\n- Scale: 1–10 (1=strong reject, 10=strong accept)\n- Accepted paper average: ~6.5; Oral average: ~7.7; Acceptance rate ~40%\n- Dimensions: Originality, Quality, Clarity, Significance, Relevance\n- CoRL papers must include a limitations section. Honestly reported limitations should be treated kindly.\n\n## Paper Summary\n**Title:** \"Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation in Variable-Rate World-Model Imagination\"\n\n**Core contribution:** A systematic diagnostic analysis of variable-rate imagination in DreamerV3, identifying a failure mode called Conditioning-Induced Value Overestimation (CIVO). When a trained temporal embedding (dt_emb) is used in imagination-based actor-critic, the RSSM's imprecise multi-step predictions create a positive feedback loop of value overestimation and policy degradation. The paper introduces Normalized Value Excess (NVE) as a diagnostic metric (AUROC=0.969 at 1.5× threshold across 18 seeds). All tested interventions fail to robustly eliminate CIVO. The contrast with TAWM (MPC-based, where dt-conditioning succeeds) localizes the vulnerability to the imagination-based actor-critic architecture.\n\n**Claims (C1-C8):**\n- C1: Variable-rate imagination consistently degrades — CONFIRMED, Strong\n- C2: Trained dt_emb → CIVO — CONFIRMED (AUROC=0.969), Strong\n- C3: Untrained dt_emb protection — CONFIRMED (6/6 seeds), Strong\n- C4: Longer Δt amplifies — CONFIRMED, Moderate\n- C5: Horizon/subsampling secondary — CONFIRMED, Moderate\n- C6: Dual vulnerability pathways — CONFIRMED (4 seeds), Strong\n- C7: Gradient ≠ causality — CONFIRMED (17 seeds), Strong\n- C8: TCN/RAVE mitigation — DROPPED (appendix negative result)\n\n**Paper Structure (12 pages):**\n- Sec 1 (Intro): Motivates variable-rate imagination, defines CIVO causal chain, 4 contributions\n- Sec 2 (Background/Related Work): \n  - Temporal abstraction in RL: options framework [Sutton et al., 1999], option-critic [Bacon et al., 2017], FiGAR [Sharma et al., 2017], dynamic action repetition\n  - World-model RL: DreamerV3 [Hafner et al., 2023], IRIS [Micheli et al., 2023], TWM [Robine et al., 2023], THICK [Kim et al., 2024], Hieros [Röpke et al., 2024], TAWM [Nhu et al., 2025]\n  - Value overestimation in MBRL: model exploitation [Janner et al., 2019], delusional bias [Lu et al., 2018], ensemble disagreement, pessimistic value estimation\n- Sec 3 (Methodology): DreamerV3 base, variable-rate extensions (dt_emb, subsampling, learned gating), factorial design, NVE metric\n- Sec 4 (Experiments): C1-C7 with tables, cross-environment results (Walker, Cheetah, Cartpole, Hopper), RSSM fidelity analysis, gradient decomposition\n- Sec 5 (Discussion): TAWM contrast, multiple pathways, gradient ≠ causality, stochastic bifurcation, implications\n- Sec 6 (Conclusion)\n- Appendix A: Comprehensive negative results (TCN, uncertainty truncation, consolidated intervention table)\n\n**References cited:** Sutton 1999, Bacon 2017, Sharma 2017, Hafner 2020/2021/2023, Micheli 2023, Robine 2023, Kim 2024, Röpke 2024, Nhu 2025, Janner 2019, Lu 2018 (12 total)\n\n**Key novelty claims:**\n1. CIVO as a new identified failure mode specific to imagination-based AC with temporal conditioning\n2. NVE as a diagnostic metric (AUROC=0.969)\n3. TAWM architectural contrast (MPC succeeds where AC fails) — localizes vulnerability\n4. Gradient magnitude ≠ causal importance (methodological insight from C7)\n5. Dual vulnerability pathways (Pathway A: value-driven, Pathway B: eval-driven)\n6. Comprehensive negative results catalog (12 intervention strategies documented)\n\n**Environments:** Walker Walk, Cheetah Run, Cartpole Swingup, Hopper Hop (all DeepMind Control Suite)\n\n## Your Review Task (R2: Novelty + Related Work)\n\nWrite a detailed review covering:\n\n1. **Summary** (2-3 sentences): Re-express the paper's main contribution\n2. **Strengths** (bulleted): Focus on novelty of the CIVO finding, positioning against prior work, TAWM contrast insight, NVE metric contribution, thoroughness of negative results\n3. **Weaknesses** (bulleted): Focus on:\n   - Missing related work (are there other papers on value overestimation in world models, temporal abstraction failures, dt-conditioning issues?)\n   - Novelty concerns (how different is CIVO from known model exploitation/delusional bias? Is the causal chain truly new?)\n   - Scope of environments (all DMC — is this sufficient for CoRL which emphasizes real robots?)\n   - Whether the paper's \"pure diagnostic\" framing is a strength or weakness for CoRL\n   - Whether 12 references is sufficient\n   - Incremental vs significant contribution assessment\n4. **Questions for authors** (bulleted)\n5. **Missing references or comparisons** — specifically look for:\n   - Other works on value overestimation in latent-dynamics models\n   - Papers on temporal abstraction in model-based RL beyond those cited\n   - Works on model exploitation mitigation (MBPO, etc.)\n   - Any papers that have already studied dt-conditioning failures\n   - Relevant robotics applications of variable-rate planning\n6. **Scores** (use CoRL 1-10 scale):\n   - Soundness\n   - Presentation\n   - Contribution\n   - Overall recommendation (1-10 with justification)\n   - Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. A diagnostic paper that identifies a fundamental failure mode can be valuable even without a solution — but assess whether the novelty is sufficient for a top venue. Consider whether this paper is more suited to CoRL (robotics focus) or a general ML venue (ICML/NeurIPS).\n\nFormat your review as a structured markdown document.","uuid":"aa9d8b8f-c605-47d7-b