{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JjWMtEzEgp1iVp7E1StN8W","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t% --- APPENDIX ---\n2\t\n3\t\\section{Complete Experimental Results}\n4\t\\label{sec:complete_results}\n5\t\n6\tTable~\\ref{tab:complete_results} reports all 31 experimental configurations. The main text (Table~\\ref{tab:main_results}) presents the subset most relevant to the causal argument; this table includes the additional variable-rate configurations (006, 007, 005), multi-seed factorial runs (009b, 008d\\_s3, 008d\\_s4), SGS interventions (018), cross-environment seeds (010b\\_s0), and mitigation experiments (008e\\_v3).\n7\t\n8\t\\begin{table*}[t]\n9\t\\centering\n10\t\\caption{Complete experimental results. NVE reports peak $\\nve$ in the 35K+ window (\\S\\ref{sec:metrics}). CIVO-positive seeds are shaded; $\\dagger$\\,marks a false positive (non-CIVO collapse with elevated NVE).}\n11\t\\label{tab:complete_results}\n12\t\\scriptsize\n13\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n14\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccccl}\n15\t\\toprule\n16\t\\textbf{Exp} & \\textbf{Config} & \\textbf{dt\\_emb} & \\textbf{Sub} & \\textbf{H} & \\textbf{Eval@final} & \\textbf{NVE} & \\textbf{Outcome} \\\\\n17\t\\midrule\n18\t001 & Baseline ($\\dt{=}1$, s1) & --- & --- & 15 & 915.7 @105K & 1.00$\\times$ & Healthy \\\\\n19\t001 s2 & Baseline ($\\dt{=}1$, s2) & --- & --- & 15 & 443.5 @105K & ---$^e$ & Healthy \\\\\n20\t\\rowcolor{gray!15}\n21\t006 & Fixed $\\dt{=}2$ & trn & ON & 15 & 132.9 @65K & 4.83$\\times$ & Collapsed \\\\\n22\t007 & Fixed $\\dt{=}4$ & trn & ON & 15 & 152.5 @55K & --- & Collapsed \\\\\n23\t005 & Learned gate & adaptive & OFF & 15 & 98.8 @145K & --- & Collapsed \\\\\n24\t\\midrule\n25\t\\rowcolor{gray!15}\n26\t008d & Factorial (s1) & trn & ON & 8 & 82.8 @55K & 4.12$\\times$ & Full collapse \\\\\n27\t\\rowcolor{gray!15}\n28\t009b & Factorial (s2) & trn & ON & 8 & 267.9 @105K & 1.62$\\times$ & Transient CIVO \\\\\n29\t\\rowcolor{gray!15}\n30\t008d\\_s3 & Factorial (s3) & trn & ON & 8 & 268$^\\diamond$ @105K & 1.53$\\times$ & Late-onset collapse \\\\\n31\t\\rowcolor{gray!15}\n32\t008d\\_s4 & Factorial (s4) & trn & ON & 8 & 63.6 @105K & 4.62$\\times$ & Full collapse \\\\\n33\t008f & No dt\\_emb (s1) & --- & ON & 8 & 503.0 @105K & 0.97$\\times$ & Healthy \\\\\n34\t008f s4 & No dt\\_emb (s4) & --- & ON & 8 & 306.5 @105K & 1.41$\\times$ & Healthy \\\\\n35\t009c s3 & No dt\\_emb (s3) & --- & ON & 8 & 311.9 @105K & 1.04$\\times$ & Healthy \\\\\n36\t008a & Unexposed emb & exp$^a$ & OFF & 15 & 777.7 @105K & 0.93$\\times$ & Healthy \\\\\n37\t008a\\_s1 & Unexposed emb & exp$^a$ & OFF & 15 & 630.9 @105K & 0.83$\\times$ & Healthy \\\\\n38\t008a\\_s2 & Unexposed emb & exp$^a$ & OFF & 15 & 899.4 @105K & 0.89$\\times$ & Healthy \\\\\n39\t015b & Frozen emb & frz$^b$ & ON & 8 & 204.2 @105K & 1.45$\\times$ & Grad.\\ starvation \\\\\n40\t022b & Frozen emb + Gumbel gate & frz$^b$ & ON & 8 & 300.7 @105K & 0.63$\\times$ & No CIVO \\\\\n41\t\\midrule\n42\t018 s1 & Full-SGS & trn$^\\ddagger$ & ON & 8 & \\sgseval{} @105K & \\sgsnve{}$\\times$$^*$ & Partial mitig.\\ (CIVO+) \\\\\n43\t\\rowcolor{gray!15}\n44\t018 s2 & Full-SGS & trn$^\\ddagger$ & ON & 8 & 40.8 @105K & --- & Collapsed \\\\\n45\t008e\\_v3 s1 & Mitigation & trn & ON & 15 & 869.9 @105K & 0.94$\\times$ & Healthy \\\\\n46\t008e\\_v3 s2$^\\dagger$ & Mitigation & trn & ON & 15 & 137.0 @105K & 3.78$\\times$ & Non-CIVO collapse \\\\\n47\t\\rowcolor{gray!15}\n48\t008e\\_v3 s3 & Mitigation & trn & ON & 15 & 92.7 @105K & 4.02$\\times$ & CIVO collapse \\\\\n49\t008e\\_v3 s4 & Mitigation & trn & ON & 15 & 899.3 @105K & 0.99$\\times$ & Healthy \\\\\n50\t\\midrule\n51\t\\multicolumn{8}{l}{\\textit{Cross-environment validation}} \\\\\n52\t\\rowcolor{gray!15}\n53\t010b & Cheetah Run & trn & ON & 8 & 51.7 @105K & 8.27$\\times$ & Collapsed \\\\\n54\t\\rowcolor{gray!15}\n55\t010b\\_s0 & Cheetah Run & trn & ON & 8 & 0.83 @105K & 108.52$\\times$ & Full collapse \\\\\n56\t\\rowcolor{gray!15}\n57\t021 & Cheetah Run & trn$^\\ddagger$ & ON & 8 & 221.5 @105K & 9.94$\\times$$^d$ & Transient CIVO (self-corr.) \\\\\n58\t024b s0 & Cheetah Run & --- & ON & 8 & 96.5 @105K & $<$0.20$\\times$ & Healthy \\\\\n59\t024b s1 & Cheetah Run & --- & ON & 8 & 77.3 @105K & 0.10$\\times$ & Healthy \\\\\n60\t008a\\_ch s0 & Cheetah Run & exp$^a$ & OFF & 15 & 532.4 @105K & 1.08$\\times$ & Healthy \\\\\n61\t008a\\_ch s1 & Cheetah Run & exp$^a$ & OFF & 15 & 504.4 @105K & 1.29$\\times$ & Healthy \\\\\n62\t\\rowcolor{gray!15}\n63\t019b & PickCube-v1 & trn & ON & 8 & 26.64 @200K & 3.78$\\times$$^c$ & Transient onset \\\\\n64\t\\bottomrule\n65\t\\end{tabular}\n66\t\\vspace{1pt}\n67\t{\\raggedright\\footnotesize\n68\ttrn = trained ($d{=}32$, subsample ON);\n69\ttrn$^\\ddagger$ = full-pathway SGS (all non-KL gradients detached);\n70\texp$^a$ = trained, subsample OFF;\n71\tfrz$^b$ = frozen ($d{=}32$, \\texttt{requires\\_grad=False});\n72\t--- = removed ($d{=}0$).\\\\\n73\t$^*$ Seed 1 only: peak NVE with transient exceedances (1.60$\\times$ at 35K, 1.66$\\times$ at 95K); self-correcting, no sustained escalation. Seed 2 collapsed.\\\\\n74\t$^\\diamond$ Transitional; train returns still declining (peak 470 to 139 over final 8K steps). Collapse had not reached equilibrium at 105K.\\\\\n75\t$^c$ NVE normalized against exp\\_019a\\_v2 (vanilla DreamerV3 on PickCube-v1).\\\\\n76\t$^d$ Peak NVE $9.94\\times$ at 45K (transient, self-correcting to $<$1$\\times$ by 55K); final eval 221.5.\\\\\n77\t$^e$ Not used for NVE normalization; reported for completeness. Seed~1 (eval 915.7) is the NVE reference baseline.\\par}\n78\t\\end{table*}\n79\t\n80\t\\section{NVE Threshold Sensitivity Analysis}\n81\t\\label{sec:appendix_sensitivity}\n82\t\n83\tWe report the full sensitivity analysis for the NVE diagnostic framework introduced in Section~\\ref{sec:results}. The main text reports Task~B2 (any CIVO detection, $\\nve \\geq 1.5\\times$). Here we additionally present Task~B1, which restricts the positive class to \\emph{sustained} CIVO, specifically experiments exhibiting persistent, accelerating value overestimation that progresses to performance collapse. Transient CIVO cases (e.g., exp~009b, which self-corrects after a brief excursion above the threshold) are classified as negative in B1.\n84\t\n85\t\\subsection{Per-Experiment NVE Values}\n86\t\n87\tTable~\\ref{tab:nve_raw} lists the NVE values at 35K+ training steps for all ten factorial experiments (including four 008e\\_v3 seeds). Experiments are sorted by descending NVE. Experiment 008e\\_v3\\_s2 exhibits elevated NVE ($3.78\\times$) due to Imagination-Reality Divergence (IRD), a mechanistically distinct phenomenon from CIVO, and is classified as negative in both tasks. Experiment 008e\\_v3\\_s3 exhibits CIVO with NVE $4.02\\times$ despite $\\Delta t{=}1$ imagination.\n88\t\n89\t\\begin{table}[ht]\n90\t\\centering\n91\t\\caption{Per-experiment NVE values (35K+ steps) and ground truth labels. $+$ = positive (CIVO), $-$ = negative. IRD = Imagination-Reality Divergence; trans.\\ = transient CIVO with self-correction.}\n92\t\\label{tab:nve_raw}\n93\t\\small\n94\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lrccc@{}}\n95\t\\toprule\n96\tExperiment & NVE & Mechanism & B1 & B2 \\\\\n97\t\\midrule\n98\t006          & 4.83 & sustained CIVO & $+$ & $+$ \\\\\n99\t008d         & 4.13 & sustained CIVO & $+$ & $+$ \\\\\n100\t008e\\_v3\\_s3 & 4.02 & CIVO           & $+$ & $+$ \\\\\n101\t008e\\_v3\\_s2 & 3.78 & IRD (non-CIVO) & $-$ & $-$ \\\\\n102\t009b         & 1.62 & transient CIVO & $-$ & $+$ \\\\\n103\t001          & 1.00 & non-CIVO       & $-$ & $-$ \\\\\n104\t008e\\_v3\\_s4 & 0.99 & non-CIVO       & $-$ & $-$ \\\\\n105\t008f         & 0.97 & non-CIVO       & $-$ & $-$ \\\\\n106\t008e\\_v3\\_s1 & 0.94 & non-CIVO       & $-$ & $-$ \\\\\n107\t008a         & 0.93 & non-CIVO       & $-$ & $-$ \\\\\n108\t\\bottomrule\n109\t\\end{tabular}\n110\t\\end{table}\n111\t\n112\t\\subsection{Multi-Threshold Classification Performance}\n113\t\n114\tTable~\\ref{tab:sensitivity_b1} reports Task~B1 classification metrics across six NVE thresholds. Task~B1 achieves $\\text{AUROC} = 1.000$ because the minimum positive NVE ($4.02\\times$, exp~008e\\_v3\\_s3) exceeds the maximum negative NVE ($3.78\\times$, exp~008e\\_v3\\_s2), yielding perfect rank separation. The optimal threshold lies in the interval $(3.78, 4.02]$; we report $\\geq 3.9\\times$ as a representative value. Table~\\ref{tab:sensitivity_b2} provides the corresponding analysis for Task~B2 ($\\text{AUROC} = 0.958$, imperfect separation due to 008e\\_v3\\_s2).\n115\t\n116\t\\begin{table*}[t]\n117\t\\centering\n118\t\\caption{\\textbf{Task~B1}: Sustained CIVO detection sensitivity analysis ($n_{\\text{pos}}=3$, $n_{\\text{neg}}=7$, $n=10$, $\\text{AUROC}=1.000$). All confidence intervals are two-sided Clopper-Pearson exact binomial ($\\alpha=0.05$). $^\\dagger$Optimal threshold in $(3.78, 4.02]$.}\n119\t\\label{tab:sensitivity_b1}\n120\t\\small\n121\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{5pt}\n122\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccccc@{}}\n123\t\\toprule\n124\tThreshold & TP & FP & FN & TN & Accuracy [95\\% CI] & Precision & Recall & F1 \\\\\n125\t\\midrule\n126\t$\\geq 1.0\\times$ & 3 & 3 & 0 & 4 & 70.0\\% \\; [34.8, 93.3] & 50.0\\% & 100\\% & 66.7\\% \\\\\n127\t$\\geq 1.2\\times$ & 3 & 2 & 0 & 5 & 80.0\\% \\; [44.4, 97.5] & 60.0\\% & 100\\% & 75.0\\% \\\\\n128\t$\\geq 1.5\\times$ & 3 & 2 & 0 & 5 & 80.0\\% \\; [44.4, 97.5] & 60.0\\% & 100\\% & 75.0\\% \\\\\n129\t\\rowcolor{gray!15}\n130\t$\\geq 1.7\\times$ & 3 & 1 & 0 & 6 & 90.0\\% \\; [55.5, 99.7] & 75.0\\% & 100\\% & 85.7\\% \\\\\n131\t$\\geq 2.0\\times$ & 3 & 1 & 0 & 6 & 90.0\\% \\; [55.5, 99.7] & 75.0\\% & 100\\% & 85.7\\% \\\\\n132\t\\rowcolor{gray!15}\n133\t$\\geq 3.9\\times^\\dagger$ & 3 & 0 & 0 & 7 & 100\\% \\; [69.2, 100] & 100\\% & 100\\% & 100\\% \\\\\n134\t\\bottomrule\n135\t\\end{tabular}\n136\t\\end{table*}\n137\t\n138\t\\begin{table*}[t]\n139\t\\centering\n140\t\\caption{\\textbf{Task~B2}: Any CIVO detection sensitivity analysis ($n_{\\text{pos}}=4$, $n_{\\text{neg}}=6$, $n=10$, $\\text{AUROC}=0.958$). The single false positive at $\\geq 1.5\\times$ is 008e\\_v3\\_s2 (IRD mechanism, not CIVO). The $1.5\\times$ threshold (highlighted) maximizes F1 while maintaining perfect recall.}\n141\t\\label{tab:sensitivity_b2}\n142\t\\small\n143\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{5pt}\n144\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccccc@{}}\n145\t\\toprule\n146\tThreshold & TP & FP & FN & TN & Accuracy [95\\% CI] & Precision & Recall & F1 \\\\\n147\t\\midrule\n148\t$\\geq 1.0\\times$ & 4 & 2 & 0 & 4 & 80.0\\% \\; [44.4, 97.5] & 66.7\\% & 100\\% & 80.0\\% \\\\\n149\t$\\geq 1.2\\times$ & 4 & 1 & 0 & 5 & 90.0\\% \\; [55.5, 99.7] & 80.0\\% & 100\\% & 88.9\\% \\\\\n150\t\\rowcolor{gray!15}\n151\t$\\geq 1.5\\times$ & 4 & 1 & 0 & 5 & 90.0\\% \\; [55.5, 99.7] & 80.0\\% & 100\\% & 88.9\\% \\\\\n152\t$\\geq 1.7\\times$ & 3 & 1 & 1 & 5 & 80.0\\% \\; [44.4, 97.5] & 75.0\\% & 75.0\\% & 75.0\\% \\\\\n153\t$\\geq 2.0\\times$ & 3 & 1 & 1 & 5 & 80.0\\% \\; [44.4, 97.5] & 75.0\\% & 75.0\\% & 75.0\\% \\\\\n154\t$\\geq 3.9\\times$ & 3 & 0 & 1 & 6 & 90.0\\% \\; [55.5, 99.7] & 100\\% & 75.0\\% & 85.7\\% \\\\\n155\t\\bottomrule\n156\t\\end{tabular}\n157\t\\end{table*}\n158\t\n159\t\\begin{table*}[t]\n160\t\\centering\n161\t\\caption{\\textbf{Task~B2 (full evaluation set)}: Any CIVO detection across two environments ($n_{\\text{pos}}=8$, $n_{\\text{neg}}=8$, $n=16$, $\\text{AUROC}=0.969$). Includes Walker Walk (14 configurations) and Cheetah Run (2 configurations).}\n162\t\\label{tab:sensitivity_b2_full}\n163\t\\small\n164\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{5pt}\n165\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lcccccccc@{}}\n166\t\\toprule\n167\tThreshold & TP & FP & FN & TN & Accuracy [95\\% CI] & Precision & Recall & F1 \\\\\n168\t\\midrule\n169\t$\\geq 1.0\\times$ & 8 & 2 & 0 & 6 & 87.5\\% \\; [61.7, 98.4] & 80.0\\% & 100\\% & 88.9\\% \\\\\n170\t$\\geq 1.2\\times$ & 8 & 1 & 0 & 7 & 93.8\\% \\; [69.8, 99.8] & 88.9\\% & 100\\% & 94.1\\% \\\\\n171\t\\rowcolor{gray!15}\n172\t$\\geq 1.5\\times$ & 8 & 1 & 0 & 7 & 93.8\\% \\; [69.8, 99.8] & 88.9\\% & 100\\% & 94.1\\% \\\\\n173\t$\\geq 1.7\\times$ & 6 & 1 & 2 & 7 & 81.3\\% \\; [54.4, 96.0] & 85.7\\% & 75.0\\% & 80.0\\% \\\\\n174\t$\\geq 2.0\\times$ & 6 & 1 & 2 & 7 & 81.3\\% \\; [54.4, 96.0] & 85.7\\% & 75.0\\% & 80.0\\% \\\\\n175\t$\\geq 3.9\\times$ & 6 & 0 & 2 & 8 & 87.5\\% \\; [61.7, 98.4] & 100\\% & 75.0\\% & 85.7\\% \\\\\n176\t\\bottomrule\n177\t\\end{tabular}\n178\t\\end{table*}\n179\t\n180\t\\subsection{Threshold Recommendation}\n181\t\n182\tFor the main diagnostic (Task~B2), we recommend $\\nve \\geq 1.5\\times$ as the operating threshold for three reasons:\n183\t\n184\t\\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=*]\n185\t\\item \\textbf{Maximum recall.} The $1.5\\times$ threshold achieves 100\\% recall, detecting all four CIVO cases including the transient case (009b, $\\nve = 1.62\\times$). Raising the threshold to $1.7\\times$ loses 009b, dropping recall to 75.0\\%.\n186\t\\item \\textbf{Best F1.} At $1.5\\times$, F1 = 88.9\\%, the highest among all tested thresholds for B2. The single false positive (008e\\_v3\\_s2) is mechanistically identifiable as IRD rather than CIVO.\n187\t\\item \\textbf{Sensitivity--specificity tradeoff.} Lower thresholds ($1.0\\times$) add false positives without gaining true positives. Higher thresholds ($\\geq 1.7\\times$) sacrifice recall without eliminating the 008e\\_v3\\_s2 false positive (which requires $\\tau > 3.78$).\n188\t\\end{enumerate}\n189\t\n190\tFor Task~B1 (sustained CIVO only), perfect classification is achievable at $\\nve \\geq 3.9\\times$, but with only $n_{\\text{pos}} = 3$ the statistical power remains limited (accuracy 95\\% CI: [69.2\\%, 100\\%]).\n191\t\n192\t\\subsection{Expanded ROC Analysis}\n193\t\\label{sec:roc_analysis}\n194\t\n195\tThe original $n{=}16$ analysis (Tables~\\ref{tab:sensitivity_b1}--\\ref{tab:sensitivity_b2_full}) predates the SGS, RAVE, and TCN experiments. We extend the NVE diagnostic evaluation to $n{=}29$ configurations spanning all tested interventions. Figure~\\ref{fig:nve_roc} shows the receiver operating characteristic curve with 95\\% bootstrap confidence interval (10{,}000 resamples).\n196\t\n197\t\\begin{figure}[h]\n198\t\\centering\n199\t\\includegraphics[width=0.7\\columnwidth]{../../artifacts/figures/nve_roc_curve.pdf}\n200\t\\caption{NVE ROC curve ($n{=}29$ configurations, AUROC${=}0.971$, 95\\% CI: 0.904--1.000). The preset $\\nve \\geq 1.5\\times$ operating point (star) achieves TPR${=}1.00$, FPR${=}0.12$. The Youden-optimal threshold $\\theta^* = 1.53$ (dashed) coincides with the preset threshold, confirming that the $1.5\\times$ cutoff is statistically principled. Two false positives arise from non-CIVO value overestimation: RAVE anchor pollution and obs-decoder SGS side effects.}\n201\t\\label{fig:nve_roc}\n202\t\\end{figure}\n203\t\n204\tWe label a configuration as CIVO-positive if and only if (i)~it uses a trained $\\dt$-embedding with multi-rate data exposure, and (ii)~NVE exceeds $1.5\\times$ with co-occurring entropy collapse. Configurations exhibiting high NVE through other mechanisms (gradient truncation side effects, value anchor pollution) are labeled non-CIVO.\n205\t\n206\tThe two false positives (FPR${=}0.12$) are mechanistically distinct from CIVO: one reflects RAVE anchor pollution (the EMA anchor itself becomes inflated under CIVO dynamics, producing overestimation through a different pathway), and the other reflects obs-decoder SGS-induced value distortion (blocking observation decoder gradients damages the shared RSSM latent space, causing systematic miscalibration). Both produce NVE $> 1.5\\times$ through non-CIVO mechanisms, indicating that the NVE diagnostic detects value overestimation broadly rather than CIVO specifically---a feature rather than a limitation for practitioners monitoring deployment health. The closest sub-threshold case (008f seed~4, NVE${=}1.41\\times$) lacks $\\dt$-conditioning entirely ($d{=}0$); its elevated NVE reflects transient value noise that self-corrects without the positive feedback loop characteristic of CIVO. The closest above-threshold positive (008d seed~3, NVE${=}1.53\\times$) exhibits the full CIVO causal chain: trained $\\dt$-embedding, entropy decrease, and evaluation collapse to 14\\% of baseline.\n207\t\n208\t\\subsection{NVE Normalization Robustness}\n209\t\\label{sec:nve_robustness}\n210\t\n211\tTo assess sensitivity to baseline seed choice, we extend the single-seed baseline (seed~1, eval 915.7 at 105K) to three additional seeds trained to 505K steps (Table~\\ref{tab:baseline_seeds}).\n212\t\n213\t\\begin{table}[h]\n214\t\\centering\n215\t\\caption{Walker Walk baseline seeds at 105K and 505K steps.}\n216\t\\label{tab:baseline_seeds}\n217\t\\footnotesize\n218\t\\begin{tabular}{lcccc}\n219\t\\toprule\n220\t\\textbf{Seed} & \\textbf{Eval@105K} & \\textbf{Eval@505K} & \\textbf{ve@65K} & \\textbf{ve@105K} \\\\\n221\t\\midrule\n222\ts1 & 915.7 & 970.4 & 0.563 & 0.656 \\\\\n223\ts2 & 443.5 & 945.7 & 0.898 & 0.742 \\\\\n224\ts3 & 869.0 & 947.7 & --- & 0.664 \\\\\n225\ts4 & 922.8 & 938.9 & --- & 0.624 \\\\\n226\t\\bottomrule\n227\t\\end{tabular}\n228\t\\end{table}\n229\t\n230\tSeed~2 exhibits slow convergence (eval 443.5 at 105K, recovering to 945.7 by 512K), confirming non-pathological slow convergence rather than seed failure. Its elevated $v/e$ ratio at 65K (0.898 vs.\\ 0.563 for seed~1) reflects the low evaluation denominator rather than pathological value overestimation. Using seed~2 as the sole normalization reference systematically deflates NVE values, causing two marginal CIVO-positive configurations (008d seed~3, peak NVE $1.53\\times$; 009b seed~2, peak NVE $1.62\\times$) to fall below the $1.5\\times$ threshold. Core CIVO cases (peak NVE $> 3\\times$) remain correctly classified under all baseline seeds.\n231\t\n232\tSingle-seed normalization (s1) yields AUROC $= 0.933$ and accuracy 7/8 (87.5\\%) on the Walker subset. Four-seed mean normalization yields AUROC $= 0.867$ and accuracy 6/8 (75.0\\%): the boundary case exp\\_009b flips from $\\nve = 1.62\\times$ (s1) to $1.23\\times$ (4-seed mean), because its peak occurs at 45K when the s1 $v/e$ ratio (0.331) is well below the 4-seed mean (0.436). All high-NVE configurations remain correctly classified (006: $4.84\\times \\to 4.52\\times$; 008d: $4.13\\times \\to 3.54\\times$). The $1.5\\times$ threshold thus correctly identifies all strong CIVO cases regardless of baseline choice; sensitivity is confined to configurations within 10\\% of the threshold.\n233\t\n234\t\\paragraph{Normalization conventions.} Walker NVE is normalized against the 4-seed mean baseline (seeds~1--4, each run to 505K+ steps). Cheetah NVE is normalized against a 5-seed mean baseline (seeds~0--4, each run to ${\\sim}$210K steps). The differing seed counts reflect available baseline data; both provide sufficient coverage of the respective experimental windows (Walker: 210K analysis window; Cheetah: 105K analysis window).\n235\t\n236\t\\paragraph{Limitations.} With $n = 10$ experiments, Clopper-Pearson confidence intervals remain wide (e.g., 90.0\\% accuracy has 95\\% CI [55.5\\%, 99.7\\%]). These results should be interpreted as a diagnostic proof-of-concept rather than a validated clinical threshold. Replication across additional environments and seeds is needed to establish tighter bounds. Table~\\ref{tab:sensitivity_b2_full} reports the original $n{=}16$ evaluation set spanning both environments. The expanded analysis ($n{=}29$, including SGS variants, RAVE, and TCN configurations) yields AUROC${=}0.971$ (95\\% CI: 0.904--1.000; Figure~\\ref{fig:nve_roc}), with the Youden-optimal threshold $\\theta^* = 1.53$ coinciding with the preset $1.5\\times$ operating point---confirming that this threshold is statistically principled rather than post-hoc.\n237\t\n238\t% --- APPENDIX B: Factorial Ablation Details ---\n239\t\n240\t\\section{Factorial Ablation Details}\n241\t\\label{sec:appendix_factorial}\n242\t\n243\tThis appendix provides the full factorial ablation results summarized in \\S\\ref{sec:f3f5_summary} of the main text. Table~\\ref{tab:factorial_design} summarizes the experimental configurations.\n244\t\n245\t\\begin{table}[ht]\n246\t\\centering\n247\t\\caption{Factorial design. Each row specifies one experimental configuration. \\textbf{Emb}: $\\dt$-embedding mode. \\textbf{Sub}: temporal subsampling. \\textbf{H}: imagination horizon. \\textbf{Imag $\\dt$}: temporal resolution used during imagination.}\n248\t\\label{tab:factorial_design}\n249\t\\small\n250\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3.5pt}\n251\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llcccc@{}}\n252\t\\toprule\n253\t\\textbf{ID} & \\textbf{Purpose} & \\textbf{Emb} & \\textbf{Sub} & \\textbf{H} & \\textbf{Imag $\\dt$} \\\\\n254\t\\midrule\n255\t001 & Reference baseline & --- & --- & 15 & 1 \\\\\n256\t006 & Fixed $\\dt{=}2$ & trn & on & 15 & 2 \\\\\n257\t007 & Fixed $\\dt{=}4$ & trn & on & 15 & 4 \\\\\n258\t\\midrule\n259\t008a & Unexposed emb ctrl & exp$^a$ & off & 15 & 2 \\\\\n260\t008c & Horizon control & exp$^a$ & off & 8 & 2 \\\\\n261\t008d & Collapse condition & trn & on & 8 & 2 \\\\\n262\t008e\\_v3 & $\\dt{=}1$ imag control & trn & on & 15 & 1 \\\\\n263\t008f & Causal ablation & --- & on & 8 & 2 \\\\\n264\t\\midrule\n265\t015b & Frozen-emb ctrl & frz$^b$ & on & 8 & 2 \\\\\n266\t016e & Stop-grad (reward) & trn$^\\dagger$ & on & 8 & 2 \\\\\n267\t018 & Full-SGS & trn$^\\ddagger$ & on & 8 & 2 \\\\\n268\t\\midrule\n269\t005 & Learned gate & adaptive & off & 15 & learned \\\\\n270\t\\bottomrule\n271\t\\end{tabular}\n272\t\\vspace{-2pt}\n273\t{\\raggedright\\footnotesize\n274\ttrn = trained ($d{=}32$, exposed to $\\dt{>}1$ data via subsample ON);\n275\texp$^a$ = trained ($d{=}32$) but never exposed to $\\dt{>}1$ data (subsample OFF);\n276\tfrz$^b$ = frozen random projection ($d{=}32$, \\texttt{requires\\_grad=False});\n277\t--- = removed ($d{=}0$).\\\\\n278\t$^\\dagger$ \\texttt{reward\\_dt\\_emb\\_detach=True}; $^\\ddagger$ all non-KL gradients to \\texttt{dt\\_emb} detached.\\par}\n279\t\\end{table}\n280\t\n281\t\\subsection{F3: Unexposed Temporal Embedding Suppresses Value Drift}\n282\t\\label{sec:f3}\n283\t\n284\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n285\t\\centering\n286\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig3_factorial_grid.pdf}\n287\t\\caption{Factorial isolation of CIVO trigger. Only the combination of trained $e_{\\dt}$ and multi-rate data (upper-left cell) produces pathological NVE. Neither factor alone is sufficient.}\n288\t\\label{fig:factorial_grid}\n289\t\\end{figure}\n290\t\n291\tThe causal claim that trained embedding \\emph{plus} multi-rate exposure jointly trigger CIVO rests on two complementary controls that each remove one factor while holding the other constant.\n292\t\n293\t\\paragraph{Evidence A: exposure is necessary (exp\\_008a).}\n294\tExp\\_008a uses a trained embedding ($d{=}32$, gradients enabled) that is never exposed to $\\dt{>}1$ transitions during world-model training (subsample OFF). Despite sharing the same embedding architecture as exp\\_008d, its NVE remains in the $0.83$--$0.93\\times$ range across 3 seeds (105K steps). Performance follows two V-shaped recovery cycles, reaching 777.7 at 105K (85\\% of baseline). The critical trigger is not the embedding's capacity to represent temporal information, but its adaptation to multi-rate data that enables precise but inaccurate $\\dt{>}1$ state predictions within the RSSM.\n295\t\n296\tThis contrasts with exp\\_006 (trained $e_{\\dt}$, subsample ON, $H{=}15$), where NVE accelerates from $1.61\\times$ at 45K to $2.31\\times$ at 55K before catastrophic collapse to 132.9 at 65K. Both experiments use identical trained embeddings ($d{=}32$, gradients enabled) and $H{=}15$, differing only in whether the world model sees $\\dt{>}1$ transitions during training. This confirms that multi-rate data exposure, not embedding architecture, is the operative variable.\n297\t\n298\t\\paragraph{Evidence B: trainability is necessary (exp\\_015b).}\n299\tExp\\_015b uses a frozen random-projection embedding ($d{=}32$, \\texttt{requires\\_grad=False}, subsample ON, $H{=}8$), the same multi-rate data regime as exp\\_008d, but with a non-trainable temporal signal. Despite full exposure to $\\dt{>}1$ transitions, the NVE peaks at $1.45\\times$ at 55K but does not sustain escalation: it retreats to $0.65\\times$ by 65K and settles at $0.42\\times$ by 105K (trajectory: $0.81 \\rightarrow 1.26 \\rightarrow 1.45 \\rightarrow 0.65 \\rightarrow 1.05 \\rightarrow 0.47 \\rightarrow 0.39 \\rightarrow 0.42$). The transient peak approaches but does not exceed the $1.5\\times$ CIVO threshold, and critically, the ratio self-corrects rather than exhibiting the monotonic escalation seen in trained-embedding runs. Final evaluation reaches only 204.2 at 105K, low but through a qualitatively different failure mode than CIVO. Actor loss approaches zero (gradient starvation; see \\S\\ref{sec:pathways}), the frozen embedding provides no useful gradient signal for temporal adaptation.\n300\t\n301\tThe contrast with exp\\_008d is stark: under identical data conditions (subsample ON, $H{=}8$), the trained embedding drives NVE to $4.12\\times$ with sustained monotonic escalation ($1.78 \\rightarrow 1.71 \\rightarrow 2.09 \\rightarrow 4.12$) and triggers full collapse (eval 82.8), while the frozen embedding peaks transiently at $1.45\\times$ before self-correcting to $0.42\\times$, a qualitative difference between sustained and transient overestimation. This demonstrates that a $\\dt$-embedding must be able to \\emph{co-adapt} with RSSM dynamics to produce the systematic reward-prediction bias that drives CIVO; a static random projection, regardless of its exposure to multi-rate data, cannot create this bias.\n302\t\n303\tTogether, these two controls establish that CIVO requires the \\emph{joint} presence of (i)~a trainable embedding that can co-adapt with RSSM dynamics, and (ii)~multi-rate training data that drives this co-adaptation toward biased temporal predictions. Neither factor alone is sufficient.\n304\t\n305\tCross-environment validation on Cheetah Run replicates the causal pattern: trained $e_{\\dt}$ triggers CIVO in both tested seeds (exp\\_010b, 2 seeds, NVE $8.27$--$108.52\\times$, collapse to 51.7/0.83) while removing the embedding eliminates it (exp\\_024b, 2 seeds, NVE $<0.2\\times$, eval 96.5/77.3). This extends the necessary-condition finding from Walker Walk to a second locomotion environment with qualitatively different dynamics (6-joint vs.\\ 2-joint).\n306\t\n307\t\\begin{table}[ht]\n308\t\\centering\n309\t\\small\n310\t\\caption{Causal isolation: $e_{\\dt}$ configuration $\\times$ multi-rate data exposure. The combination of trained embedding and multi-rate exposure is the sole trigger for CIVO. Neither factor alone is sufficient.}\n311\t\\label{tab:causal_matrix}\n312\t\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n313\t\\toprule\n314\t& Multi-rate data (\\textsc{on}) & Single-rate data (\\textsc{off}) \\\\\n315\t\\midrule\n316\tTrained $e_{\\dt}$ & exp\\_008d: \\textbf{CIVO} & exp\\_008a: healthy \\\\\n317\t & NVE 4.12$\\times$, collapse & NVE 0.93$\\times$ \\\\\n318\t\\midrule\n319\tFrozen $e_{\\dt}$ & exp\\_015b: grad.\\ starvation & --- \\\\\n320\t & peak NVE 1.45$\\times$ (transient) & \\\\\n321\t\\midrule\n322\tRemoved $e_{\\dt}$ & exp\\_008f: no CIVO & --- \\\\\n323\t & NVE $\\leq$0.97$\\times$ & \\\\\n324\t\\bottomrule\n325\t\\end{tabular}\n326\t\\end{table}\n327\t\n328\tOnly the upper-left cell (trained embedding with multi-rate exposure) triggers CIVO (Table~\\ref{tab:causal_matrix}). This 2$\\times$2 structure establishes that both factors are jointly necessary.\n329\t\n330\t\\subsection{F4: Degradation Scales Monotonically with \\texorpdfstring{$\\dt$}{dt}}\n331\t\\label{sec:f4}\n332\t\n333\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n334\t\\centering\n335\t\\includegraphics[width=\\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig5_dt_amplification.pdf}\n336\t\\caption{Peak evaluation return as a function of imagination $\\dt$. Performance degrades monotonically: $\\dt{=}1$ (915.7) $>$ $\\dt{=}2$ (485.3, $-47\\%$) $>$ $\\dt{=}4$ (176.9, $-81\\%$).}\n337\t\\label{fig:dt_amplification}\n338\t\\end{figure}\n339\t\n340\tThe magnitude of degradation scales monotonically with the imagination step size (Figure~\\ref{fig:dt_amplification}). From the $\\dt{=}1$ baseline (915.7 at 105K), fixed $\\dt{=}2$ (exp\\_006) achieves a peak of only 485.3 ($-47\\%$), and fixed $\\dt{=}4$ (exp\\_007) peaks at 176.9 ($-81\\%$). This monotonic relationship is consistent with compounding model error: larger temporal gaps amplify the RSSM's prediction inaccuracy at each imagination step, and these errors accumulate across the rollout horizon.\n341\t\n342\t\\subsection{F5: Imagination Horizon and Subsampling Are Secondary Factors}\n343\t\\label{sec:f5}\n344\t\n345\tNeither horizon length nor temporal subsampling triggers collapse without multi-rate data exposure: both trained-but-unexposed configurations (008a, 008c) maintain $\\nve < 0.93\\times$ (\\S\\ref{sec:f3}), while the trained-and-exposed counterpart (008d) collapses with $\\nve = 4.12\\times$.  The no-embedding configurations show cross-seed variance (008f: 503.0 vs.\\ 009c: 148.3) but neither exhibits $\\nve > 1.5\\times$; low evaluation alone does not indicate CIVO.\n346\t\n347\tThe $3.2\\times$ performance gap (008a: 777.7 vs.\\ 008c: 240.9) quantifies the horizon effect independently of CIVO, as both conditions maintain healthy NVE ($\\leq$0.93$\\times$).\n348\t\n349\t% --- APPENDIX C: Full Analysis & Discussion ---\n350\t\n351\t\\section{Full Analysis \\& Discussion}\n352\t\\label{sec:appendix_analysis}\n353\t\n354\tThis appendix provides the complete mechanistic analysis summarized in Section~\\ref{sec:discussion} of the main text.\n355\t\n356\t\\subsection{The CIVO Mechanism}\n357\t\\label{sec:civo_mechanism}\n358\t\n359\tThe RSSM fidelity analysis (Table~\\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}) reveals the mechanistic core of CIVO.  The trained $\\dt$-embedding enables the reward head to learn temporally differentiated predictions, but these predictions are asymmetrically accurate: reward MAE at $\\dt{=}1$ is $0.0568$, while at $\\dt{=}2$ it rises to $0.1579$, a $2.78\\times$ gap.  This gap is not an artifact of prediction difficulty alone; the no-embedding configuration (008f) exhibits a $1.01\\times$ gap, and the baseline shows $1.56\\times$.  The trained embedding actively amplifies the fidelity discrepancy by allowing the reward head to co-adapt with the temporal signal.\n360\t\n361\tThis asymmetric fidelity initiates a feedback loop with four stages: (1) the trained $\\dt$-embedding causes RSSM predictions at $\\dt > 1$ to carry systematically biased reward estimates; (2) the critic, trained entirely on imagined trajectories, internalizes these biased rewards as optimistic value estimates; (3) the actor exploits the overestimated regions of the value landscape, shifting its policy toward states that appear high-value only under the biased model; and (4) this policy shift further skews the distribution of imagined states, reinforcing the bias.  The diagnostic signature is a sustained $\\nve$ above $1.5\\times$, followed by evaluation collapse.\n362\t\n363\tA random-policy control experiment ($N{=}100$ rollouts, 95\\% bootstrap CI) decomposes CIVO into two necessary stages.  \\textbf{Stage~1 (dynamics bias):} even under a random policy, $\\dt{=}2$ imagination produces $1.68\\times$ higher mean reward than $\\dt{=}1$ in the trained-embedding model (008d): the RSSM prior already generates reward-favorable trajectories at $\\dt > 1$, independent of actor behavior.  \\textbf{Stage~2 (actor exploitation):} the trained actor learns to exploit these biased regions, amplifying the ratio to $2.90\\times$.  In the no-embedding control (008f), neither stage is present: the random-policy ratio is $1.11\\times$ and the trained-actor ratio is $0.98\\times$.  The decomposition attributes $\\approx 36\\%$ of the total effect to dynamics bias and $\\approx 64\\%$ to actor exploitation, which confirms that both stages are necessary for CIVO to manifest.\n364\t\n365\tThis mechanism is analogous to delusional bias \\citep{lu2018nondelusional}, but differs in origin: delusional bias arises from policy-class constraints, while CIVO is model-induced: the reward head's co-adaptation with the temporal embedding produces systematically upward-biased predictions at $\\dt > 1$.  The consequence (policy exploitation of overestimated regions) is similar, but the causal pathway is distinct.\n366\t\n367\t\\subsection{Selective Gradient Stopping and Gradient Decomposition}\n368\t\\label{sec:sgs_gradient_analysis}\n369\t\n370\t\\subsubsection{Gradient Decomposition Results}\n371\t\\label{sec:gradient_decomposition}\n372\t\n373\t\\begin{figure}[t]\n374\t\\centering\n375\t\\includegraphics[width=0.75\\columnwidth]{figures/fig3_gradient_decomposition.pdf}\n376\t\\caption{Gradient contribution to $e_{\\Delta t}$ by loss component at two training checkpoints. The reward head contributes the largest share (38--49\\%) yet reward-only SGS (exp\\_016e) fails; the observation decoder channel (16--22\\%) carries the critical $\\dt$-specific spatial information that drives the RSSM fidelity gap.}\n377\t\\label{fig:gradient_decomp}\n378\t\\end{figure}\n379\t\n380\tExp\\_016e collapses in the same qualitative pattern as the unmodified control: peak evaluation 326.7 at 75K steps followed by catastrophic decline to 31.7 at 95K, with NVE reaching $10.42\\times$. The SGS intervention neither prevented nor attenuated CIVO.\n381\t\n382\tTo explain this null result, we performed a gradient decomposition analysis using \\texttt{torch.autograd.grad} to measure each loss component's contribution to $\\nabla_\\theta e_{\\Delta t}$, where $\\theta$ denotes the temporal embedding parameters. Table~\\ref{tab:gradient_decomposition} reports the results.\n383\t\n384\t\\begin{table}[ht]\n385\t\\centering\n386\t\\caption{Gradient contributions to the temporal embedding $e_{\\Delta t}$ by loss component, measured via \\texttt{torch.autograd.grad} with training configuration \\texttt{gate\\_fixed\\_dt=2}. Gradient norms are $\\ell_2$ norms of $\\nabla_\\theta \\mathcal{L}_i$ for each loss $\\mathcal{L}_i$.}\n387\t\\label{tab:gradient_decomposition}\n388\t\\small\n389\t\\begin{tabular}{lrrrr}\n390\t\\toprule\n391\t& \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{@55K}} & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{\\textbf{@112K}} \\\\\n392\t\\cmidrule(lr){2-3} \\cmidrule(lr){4-5}\n393\tLoss component & Share & (\\%) & Share & (\\%) \\\\\n394\t\\midrule\n395\tReward head & --- & 49.0 & --- & 38.1 \\\\\n396\tKL dynamics & --- & 34.7 & --- & 40.4 \\\\\n397\tObservation decoders & --- & 16.3 & --- & 21.5 \\\\\n398\t\\bottomrule\n399\t\\end{tabular}\n400\t\\end{table}\n401\t\n402\tThe gradient decomposition reveals a counterintuitive structure: the reward head provides the largest gradient contribution (49.0\\% at 55K, 38.1\\% at 112K), yet reward-only SGS (exp\\_016e) fails catastrophically (NVE $10.42\\times$). The observation decoder channel contributes the smallest share (16.3\\% at 55K, 21.5\\% at 112K) yet carries the critical $\\dt$-specific spatial information.  This dissociation between gradient magnitude and causal importance explains why exp\\_016e, which targets the largest pathway, is functionally insufficient for CIVO prevention.\n403\t\n404\tThe gradient structure is relatively stable across training (reward shifts from 49\\% to 38\\%, obs from 16\\% to 22\\%, KL from 35\\% to 40\\%), unlike what a magnitude-based account would predict. The reward head's large gradient contribution reflects the scalar reward prediction task's sensitivity to temporal embedding parameters, but this gradient does not encode $\\Delta t$-specific structural information about spatial dynamics. The observation decoder channel, despite its smaller magnitude, is the only pathway that encodes how the RSSM should differentially predict multi-dimensional proprioceptive states across temporal scales.\n405\t\n406\t\\paragraph{Why the smallest channel is causally critical.} The observation decoder is the only loss component whose gradients encode how the RSSM should differentially predict multi-dimensional proprioceptive states ($14$ orientations, $9$ velocities, $1$ height) across temporal scales.  This $\\dt$-specific spatial information shapes the latent state representation in ways that propagate to reward prediction: the RSSM fidelity gap ($2.78\\times$ reward MAE ratio, Table~\\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}) arises because the temporal embedding learns to produce latent states that are spatially biased at $\\dt{>}1$, not because the reward head directly learns scale-dependent biases.  Full-SGS targets this causal chain by preventing observation-decoder gradients from updating $e_{\\Delta t}$, though its 0/2 CIVO elimination rate on Walker (seed~1: NVE reduced from $4.12\\times$ to $1.66\\times$ but remains CIVO-positive; seed~2: collapsed) indicates that breaking the gradient pathway alone does not fully eliminate the vulnerability.\n407\t\n408\t\\subsubsection{Implications for the CIVO Mechanism}\n409\t\\label{sec:sgs_implications}\n410\t\n411\tThe gradient decomposition reveals that gradient magnitude and causal importance are decoupled in CIVO.\n412\t\n413\t\\paragraph{Gradient importance $\\neq$ gradient magnitude.} The reward head contributes the largest gradient share (38--49\\%) to $e_{\\Delta t}$, yet reward-only SGS fails.  The observation decoder contributes the smallest share (16--22\\%), yet its removal (via Full-SGS) is necessary for CIVO mitigation.  This dissociation arises because the reward head's gradient, while large, does not encode $\\dt$-specific structural information: it adjusts $e_{\\Delta t}$ to minimize a scalar prediction error that is largely $\\dt$-invariant.  The observation decoder's gradient, though smaller, encodes how the RSSM should differentially reconstruct 24-dimensional proprioceptive states across temporal scales. This is the channel through which $e_{\\Delta t}$ learns scale-dependent latent biases.\n414\t\n415\t\\paragraph{Reframing the intervention target.} Any gradient-level strategy that targets the reward head alone (reward prediction regularization, reward-conditioned value clipping, or reward-only SGS) targets the largest pathway but not the informationally critical one.  The failure of exp\\_016e (NVE $10.42\\times$) despite detaching the dominant gradient source confirms this.  Full-pathway SGS (exp\\_018) detaches $e_{\\Delta t}$ from all non-KL gradients, retaining only the KL dynamics contribution (34--40\\%).  However, this intervention eliminates CIVO in neither seed: seed~1 shows partial mitigation (eval 448.3, peak NVE $1.66\\times$, still CIVO-positive) while seed~2 collapses (eval 40.8). Removing the observation decoder gradient channel is thus contributory but not sufficient. Figure~\\ref{fig:civo_decomposition} decomposes evaluation returns across conditions.\n416\t\n417\t\\begin{figure}[ht]\n418\t\\centering\n419\t\\includegraphics[width=0.75\\columnwidth]{figures/fig2_civo_decomposition.pdf}\n420\t\\caption{CIVO decomposition: peak and final (105K step) evaluation returns on Walker Walk across four conditions. \\textbf{Baseline} (001): vanilla DreamerV3. \\textbf{Trained dt-emb} (008d)\\protect\\footnotemark: learned $d{=}32$ temporal embedding with multi-rate data. \\textbf{Full-SGS} (s1, s2): trained embedding with all non-KL gradients detached from $e_{\\dt}$. Trained $\\dt$-embedding degrades peak eval by $73\\%$ and final eval by $86\\%$. Full-SGS seed~1 shows partial mitigation ($-51\\%$ final, NVE $1.66\\times$, still CIVO-positive) while seed~2 collapses ($-96\\%$ final). CIVO elimination rate $= 0/2$.}\n421\t\\label{fig:civo_decomposition}\n422\t\\end{figure}\n423\t\\footnotetext{Experiment 008d terminated at 55K steps; the 105K data point uses continuation run 009e under identical configuration.}\n424\t\n425\tCross-environment validation on Cheetah Run (exp\\_021) further clarifies the role of Full-SGS.  The intervention did not prevent the initial CIVO onset: NVE peaked at $9.94\\times$ at 45K (comparable to the unmitigated exp\\_010b's $8.27\\times$).  However, unlike exp\\_010b, which collapsed permanently to eval 51.7, exp\\_021 self-corrected within ${\\sim}$10K steps, with sustained NVE dropping to $0.34\\times$ (55K--105K) and final evaluation recovering to 221.5.  This transient self-correcting trajectory is not attributable to the SGS intervention: the initial CIVO onset and peak severity are comparable to the unmitigated case, and the self-correction may reflect Cheetah Run's simpler dynamics rather than SGS efficacy.  Performance remains at 38.7\\% of baseline (572.5), likely because Full-SGS over-aggressively blocks actor gradients (actor gradient norms near zero throughout training).  Combined with exp\\_018's Walker results (0/2 CIVO elimination), these findings yield a 0/3 elimination rate across environments, indicating that gradient-level interventions alone do not eliminate the CIVO vulnerability. Additional mechanisms (e.g., forward-pass conditioning effects, residual capacity-dependent instabilities) operate beyond the gradient pathway.\n426\t\n427\t\\paragraph{Connection to RSSM fidelity analysis.} The trained-embedding condition (exp\\_008d) shows a $2.78\\times$ reward gap between $\\Delta t{=}1$ and $\\Delta t{=}2$ transitions (Table~\\ref{tab:rssm_fidelity}).  This gap arises because the observation decoder gradients shape $e_{\\Delta t}$ to produce latent states that are spatially biased at $\\dt{>}1$, and the reward head inherits these biases \\textit{through the latent state} rather than learning them directly.  The reward head's large gradient contribution reflects sensitivity to $e_{\\Delta t}$'s parameters but not causal responsibility for the fidelity gap.\n428\t\n429\t\\subsubsection{Entropy Dynamics Across Configurations}\n430\t\\label{sec:entropy_dynamics}\n431\t\n432\t\\begin{figure}[ht]\n433\t\\centering\n434\t\\includegraphics[width=0.85\\columnwidth]{figures/fig4_entropy_dynamics.pdf}\n435\t\\caption{Actor entropy trajectories for three configurations. All start at ${\\sim}8$ nats and converge to similar values (${\\sim}{-}4.5$ to ${-}5$). The CIVO-triggering configuration (008d, red) follows a normal entropy reduction trajectory; the divergence from the control (008f) is subtle in entropy space, consistent with CIVO operating through the value channel rather than the entropy channel. 018v2 (Full-SGS, seed~1) shows slightly lower terminal entropy.}\n436\t\\label{fig:entropy_dynamics}\n437\t\\end{figure}\n438\t\n439\t\\subsubsection{Exp\\_016e Reinterpreted as Stochastic Variation}\n440\t\\label{sec:016e_reinterp}\n441\t\n442\tThe gradient decomposition establishes that exp\\_016e targets the largest gradient pathway (reward: 38--49\\%) but not the informationally critical one (observation decoders: 16--22\\%).  The apparent timing differences between exp\\_016e and exp\\_008d (later collapse onset, 75K vs.\\ 25K, and higher peak evaluation, 326.7 vs.\\ 167.2) must be attributed to stochastic variation rather than SGS effects, since the critical observation decoder channel remains intact.  All three trajectories (exp\\_008d, exp\\_009b, exp\\_016e) share the invariant identified by the NVE diagnostic: each crosses the $1.5\\times$ pathological threshold at some point during training.\n443\t\n444\t\\subsection{Post-CIVO Outcomes: Why Seeds Diverge}\n445\t\\label{sec:seed_divergence}\n446\t\n447\t\\begin{table}[ht]\n448\t\\centering\n449\t\\caption{Post-CIVO outcomes for all six CIVO-positive seeds across two environments. Peak/Final NVE measured in the 35K+ window.}\n450\t\\label{tab:post_civo}\n451\t\\footnotesize\n452\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n453\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccl}\n454\t\\toprule\n455\t\\textbf{Env} & \\textbf{Seed} & \\textbf{Peak} & \\textbf{Final} & \\textbf{Eval} & \\textbf{Outcome} \\\\\n456\t\\midrule\n457\tWalker & s1 (008d) & 4.12$\\times$ & 4.12$\\times$ & 82.8 & Full collapse \\\\\n458\tWalker & s2 (009b) & 1.62$\\times$ & 0.38$\\times$ & 267.9 & Transient CIVO \\\\\n459\tWalker & s3 (008d\\_s3) & 1.53$\\times$ & 1.53$\\times$ & 268$^\\diamond$ & Late-onset collapse \\\\\n460\tWalker & s4 (008d\\_s4) & 4.62$\\times$ & 4.62$\\times$ & 63.6 & Full collapse \\\\\n461\t\\midrule\n462\tCheetah & s1 (010b) & 8.27$\\times$ & 8.27$\\times$ & 51.7 & Full collapse \\\\\n463\tCheetah & s0 (010b\\_s0) & 108.52$\\times$ & 108.52$\\times$ & 0.83 & Full collapse \\\\\n464\t\\bottomrule\n465\t\\end{tabular}\n466\t\\vspace{1pt}\n467\t{\\raggedright\\footnotesize $^\\diamond$ Transitional; train returns still declining at 105K.\\par}\n468\t\\end{table}\n469\t\n470\tAll six CIVO-positive seeds cross the $\\nve > 1.5\\times$ threshold (Table~\\ref{tab:post_civo}), yet the downstream damage is stochastic.\\footnote{Exp\\_009e was excluded from multi-seed analyses due to a seed configuration error (default seed=0, identical to exp\\_008d).}  Full collapse is the majority outcome (5/6 seeds), with one self-correction case (Walker seed~2) that remains severely degraded (29.3\\% of baseline). Cheetah seeds exhibit more extreme overestimation (peak NVE $8.27$--$108.52\\times$ vs.\\ Walker's $1.53$--$4.62\\times$), consistent with the Cheetah reward scale amplifying the fidelity gap.  We hypothesize that the divergence relates to the critic's representational capacity at the time of CIVO onset.  \\citet{lyle2022capacity} demonstrate that deep RL networks suffer progressive capacity loss, in which early-learned features become increasingly difficult to overwrite.  If the critic's capacity is already partially consumed when CIVO injects overestimation, the network cannot recalibrate (seed~0); if sufficient capacity remains, the critic can absorb transient overestimation and recover (seed~2).\n471\t\n472\t\\paragraph{A fourth trajectory: CIVO-induced persistent value collapse.}\n473\tObs-decoder SGS seed~3 (exp\\_025) exhibits a trajectory not observed in the untreated seeds: healthy through 105K ($\\nve < 1.0\\times$, eval 818.6), CIVO onset at 135K ($\\nve = 5.47\\times$, eval 67.0), followed by evaluation recovery (590.2 at 215K, 64\\% of baseline) but persistent value collapse ($\\nve = 0.14\\times$, value mean 80.6). The critic undershoots after the CIVO episode, suggesting that CIVO-induced representational damage may be irreversible even when the policy partially recovers. This contrasts with transient CIVO (seed~2, 009b), where both value and evaluation recover jointly, and with full collapse (seed~1, 008d), where neither recovers. Seed~4 exhibits the same terminal value collapse ($\\nve = 0.14\\times$ at 215K, eval 242), suggesting this overcorrection is systematic rather than seed-specific. The pattern adds a fourth post-CIVO outcome---policy recovery with critic damage---to the three documented in Table~\\ref{tab:post_civo}.\n474\t\n475\t\\subsubsection{Failure and Degradation Mode Taxonomy}\n476\t\n477\tTable~\\ref{tab:failure_modes} classifies the seven distinct failure and degradation modes observed across all experimental configurations. The taxonomy reveals that CIVO is not a single failure mode but a family of related pathologies, with the trained $\\dt$-embedding creating vulnerability through at least three independent mechanisms: value-driven collapse (monotonic, late-onset, and SGS-resistant CIVO), entropy-driven instability (IRD collapse), and gradient-mediated effects (gradient starvation, SGS-paradoxical worsening).\n478\t\n479\t\\begin{table}[ht]\n480\t\\centering\n481\t\\caption{Failure and degradation mode taxonomy. Each mode is characterized by its NVE trajectory, entropy behavior, and primary mechanism.}\n482\t\\label{tab:failure_modes}\n483\t\\footnotesize\n484\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n485\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}llccl@{}}\n486\t\\toprule\n487\t\\textbf{Mode} & \\textbf{Representative} & \\textbf{NVE} & \\textbf{Entropy} & \\textbf{Mechanism} \\\\\n488\t\\midrule\n489\tMonotonic CIVO & 008d, 008d\\_s4, 010b\\_s0 & $\\uparrow$ sustained & $\\downarrow$ & Reward bias $\\to$ critic $\\to$ actor \\\\\n490\tLate-onset CIVO & 008d\\_s3 & $\\uparrow$ delayed & $\\downarrow$ & Same, delayed onset \\\\\n491\tTransient CIVO & 009b & Peak $\\to$ $\\downarrow$ & Stable & Self-correction via capacity \\\\\n492\tIRD collapse & 008e\\_v3\\_s2 & $\\uparrow$ late & $\\uparrow$ & Entropy-driven, values stable \\\\\n493\tGrad.\\ starvation & 015b & Oscillates, decays & Stable & Frozen emb $\\to$ zero actor grad \\\\\n494\tSGS-resistant & 018\\_s2 & $\\uparrow$ & $\\downarrow$ & Forward-pass conditioning \\\\\n495\tSGS-paradoxical & 016e & $\\uparrow$ sustained & $\\downarrow$ & Reward SGS $\\to$ obs-dec reliance \\\\\n496\t\\bottomrule\n497\t\\end{tabular}\n498\t\\end{table}\n499\t\n500\t\\subsection{Why MPC Is Immune}\n501\t\\label{sec:mpc_immune}\n502\t\n503\t\\citet{nhu2025tawm} show that $\\dt$-conditioning benefits TD-MPC2, raising the question of why it fails in DreamerV3.  The critical difference is that MPC re-plans from real states at every step, so imagination errors do not accumulate into the training signal because there is no critic bootstrapping from imagined trajectories.  In DreamerV3, the actor-critic trains entirely within imagination, and any systematic reward-prediction bias at $\\dt > 1$ is amplified through the closed actor-critic loop.\n504\t\n505\t\\subsection{The \\texorpdfstring{$\\dt$}{dt}-Embedding Dilemma}\n506\t\\label{sec:embedding_dilemma}\n507\t\n508\tThe factorial results reveal a fundamental tension: removing the $\\dt$-embedding (008f) eliminates CIVO but at a $13\\times$ cost to reward prediction quality ($0.7469$ vs.\\ $0.0568$ MAE), as the world model cannot distinguish temporal scales.  Restricting imagination to $\\dt{=}1$ while retaining the trained embedding (008e\\_v3) avoids collapse in 2 of 4 seeds (50\\%), while the remaining two collapse via distinct mechanisms: Pathway~A (CIVO-driven, entropy decrease; s3, NVE $4.02\\times$) and Pathway~B (entropy-driven, entropy increase; s2, NVE $3.78\\times$; \\S\\ref{sec:f6}).  Neither approach alone provides a complete solution.\n509\t\n510\t\\subsection{Multiple Vulnerability Pathways}\n511\t\\label{sec:pathways}\n512\t\n513\tThe two collapse mechanisms identified in \\S\\ref{sec:f6}, CIVO (Pathway~A, value-driven, entropy decreasing) and non-CIVO instability (Pathway~B, eval-driven, entropy increasing), have distinct diagnostic signatures but a shared root cause: the trained $\\dt$-embedding's learned features affect both reward prediction across temporal scales (Pathway~A) and the representation space in which the actor operates (Pathway~B).  This explains why $\\nve$ is sensitive but not perfectly specific: Pathway~B's late evaluation decline inflates $\\nve$ through the same arithmetic as CIVO, yielding the sole false positive ($3.78\\times$ for 008e\\_v3 s2).  Distinguishing the two requires examining the temporal profile and entropy direction of collapse (Figure~\\ref{fig:multiple_pathways}).\n514\t\n515\t\\begin{figure}[ht]\n516\t\\centering\n517\t\\includegraphics[width=0.75\\columnwidth]{figures/paper/fig6_multiple_pathways.pdf}\n518\t\\caption{Contrasting collapse dynamics. \\textbf{Left:} 008d (CIVO), value explosion with entropy decrease. \\textbf{Right:} 008e\\_v3 s2 (non-CIVO), evaluation crash with entropy increase.}\n519\t\\label{fig:multiple_pathways}\n520\t\\end{figure}\n521\t\n522\t\\subsection{Implications for Temporal Abstraction in World Models}\n523\t\\label{sec:implications}\n524\t\n525\tCIVO identifies a structural incompatibility between flat temporal conditioning and imagination-based actor-critic training.  Hierarchical approaches (THICK \\citep{gumbsch2024thick}, Hieros \\citep{mattes2024hieros}, LS-Imagine \\citep{li2025lsimagine}) may avoid this by maintaining separate dynamics per temporal scale.  Whether CIVO transfers to transformer-based world models (DreamerV4; \\citealt{hafner2025dreamerv4}) depends on whether attention weights co-adapt with temporal embeddings analogously.  The CIVO mechanism requires three structural properties: (1) a temporal embedding that co-adapts with dynamics via gradient flow, (2) multi-step imagined rollouts used for critic training, and (3) a reward head that inherits latent biases from the shared representation.  Transformer-based world models that share these properties may exhibit analogous vulnerability; architectures that decouple temporal conditioning from the reward prediction pathway would not.\n526\t\n527\t\\subsection{Cross-Environment TCN Evaluation}\n528\t\\label{sec:tcn_cross_env}\n529\t\n530\tTable~\\ref{tab:tcn_full} reports the full TCN evaluation across environments and noise magnitudes.\n531\t\n532\t\\begin{table}[h]\n533\t\\centering\n534\t\\caption{TCN results across environments and noise magnitudes. Prevention = peak NVE $< 1.5\\times$ (35K+).}\n535\t\\label{tab:tcn_full}\n536\t\\footnotesize\n537\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{4pt}\n538\t\\begin{tabular}{llcccc}\n539\t\\toprule\n540\t\\textbf{Env} & $\\sigma$ & \\textbf{Seed} & \\textbf{NVE$_{\\max}$} & \\textbf{Prevented?} & \\textbf{Eval (\\%)} \\\\\n541\t\\midrule\n542\tWalker & 0.2 & s0 & 0.91 & \\checkmark & 91\\% \\\\\n543\tWalker & 0.2 & s1 & 0.64 & \\checkmark & 45\\% \\\\\n544\tWalker & 0.5 & s0 & 3.97 & $\\times$ & ${\\sim}40\\%$ \\\\\n545\tWalker & 0.5 & s1 & 1.64 & $\\times$ & ${\\sim}40\\%$ \\\\\n546\t\\midrule\n547\tCheetah & 0.2 & s0 & 1.90 & $\\times$ & crashed \\\\\n548\tCheetah & 0.5 & s0 & 10.67 & $\\times$ & 26\\% \\\\\n549\t\\midrule\n550\tPickCube & 0.2 & s0 & 3.71 & $\\times$ & failing \\\\\n551\t\\bottomrule\n552\t\\end{tabular}\n553\t\\end{table}\n554\t\n555\t\\paragraph{Cheetah Run $\\sigma{=}0.5$: transient spike with permanent eval damage.}\n556\tCheetah $\\sigma{=}0.5$ exhibits a novel failure mode: a transient CIVO spike (NVE $= 10.67\\times$ at 55K) followed by NVE recovery ($< 0.3\\times$ by 95K), but permanent evaluation damage (26\\% of baseline). The value signal self-corrects while the policy does not recover, suggesting that CIVO-induced representational damage during the spike is irreversible even when the critic subsequently recalibrates. This trajectory parallels the obs-decoder SGS seed~3 pattern (\\S\\ref{sec:seed_divergence}), where partial policy recovery coexists with persistent critic damage.\n557\t\n558\t\\paragraph{Cheetah Run $\\sigma{=}0.2$: insufficient dose.}\n559\tAt $\\sigma{=}0.2$, Cheetah reaches NVE $= 1.9\\times$ at 55K---exceeding the $1.5\\times$ threshold---consistent with Cheetah's higher baseline CIVO severity ($8$--$108\\times$ vs.\\ Walker's $2$--$6\\times$). The noise magnitude that suffices for Walker is inadequate for Cheetah, but increasing $\\sigma$ to 0.5 trades CIVO prevention for a different failure mode rather than achieving mitigation.\n560\t\n561\t\\paragraph{PickCube-v1 $\\sigma{=}0.2$: sparse-reward failure.}\n562\tPickCube reaches NVE $= 3.71\\times$ at 52.5K with $\\sigma{=}0.2$, indicating that TCN provides no protection in sparse-reward manipulation tasks. The combination of sparse rewards and temporal conditioning may amplify the fidelity gap beyond what representation-level noise can attenuate.\n563\t\n564\t\\section{DreamerV3 Hyperparameters}\n565\t\\label{sec:hyperparameters}\n566\t\n567\tTable~\\ref{tab:hyperparams} lists the DreamerV3 hyperparameters used across all experiments. Unless otherwise noted, we use the default DreamerV3 configuration for continuous-control tasks (\\texttt{dmc\\_proprio}).\n568\t\n569\t\\begin{table}[ht]\n570\t\\centering\n571\t\\caption{DreamerV3 hyperparameters for all experiments. Modifications from defaults are marked with $\\diamond$.}\n572\t\\label{tab:hyperparams}\n573\t\\small\n574\t\\begin{tabular}{ll}\n575\t\\toprule\n576\t\\textbf{Parameter} & \\textbf{Value} \\\\\n577\t\\midrule\n578\t\\multicolumn{2}{l}{\\textit{World Model}} \\\\\n579\tRSSM discrete states & 32 classes $\\times$ 32 categories \\\\\n580\tRSSM stochastic dim & 32 \\\\\n581\tRSSM deterministic dim & 512 \\\\\n582\tMLP hidden size & 512 \\\\\n583\tMLP layers & 2 \\\\\n584\tActivation & SiLU \\\\\n585\tNormalization & LayerNorm \\\\\n586\t\\midrule\n587\t\\multicolumn{2}{l}{\\textit{Training}} \\\\\n588\tBatch size & 16 sequences $\\times$ 64 steps \\\\\n589\tLearning rate & $3 \\times 10^{-4}$ (Adam, $\\epsilon{=}10^{-8}$) \\\\\n590\tSequence length & 64 \\\\\n591\tTraining ratio & 512 \\\\\n592\tFree nats & 1.0 \\\\\n593\tKL balance ($\\alpha$) & 0.8 \\\\\n594\tDiscount ($\\gamma$) & 0.997 \\\\\n595\t$\\lambda$-return & 0.95 \\\\\n596\t\\midrule\n597\t\\multicolumn{2}{l}{\\textit{Policy}} \\\\\n598\tImagination horizon$^\\diamond$ & 8 or 15 (see Table~\\ref{tab:complete_results}) \\\\\n599\tActor entropy scale & $3 \\times 10^{-4}$ \\\\\n600\tActor gradient & Reinforce + dynamics backprop \\\\\n601\t\\midrule\n602\t\\multicolumn{2}{l}{\\textit{Temporal Abstraction}$^\\diamond$} \\\\\n603\tGate enabled & True (all non-baseline) \\\\\n604\tGate fixed $\\dt$ & 1 or 2 (per config) \\\\\n605\tSubsample max $\\dt$ & 1 (OFF) or 2 (ON) \\\\\n606\t$\\dt$-embedding dim & 32 \\\\\n607\t\\midrule\n608\t\\multicolumn{2}{l}{\\textit{Environment}} \\\\\n609\tAction repeat & 2 (Walker Walk, Cheetah Run) \\\\\n610\tObservation & Proprioceptive (no pixels) \\\\\n611\tTotal environment steps & 105K \\\\\n612\tEvaluation frequency & Every 10K steps \\\\\n613\tEvaluation episodes & 10 \\\\\n614\t\\bottomrule\n615\t\\end{tabular}\n616\t\\end{table}\n617\t\n618\t\\section{Online CIVO Detector Details}\n619\t\\label{sec:detector_appendix}\n620\t\n621\tThis appendix provides the full algorithm specification and validation results for the online CIVO detector described in \\S\\ref{sec:detector}.\n622\t\n623\t\\subsection{Algorithm Specification}\n624\t\n625\t\\begin{algorithm}[ht]\n626\t\\caption{Online CIVO Detector}\n627\t\\label{alg:civo_detector}\n628\t\\begin{algorithmic}[1]\n629\t\\REQUIRE Stream of $(\\bar{V}_t, \\bar{E}_t)$ at each checkpoint $t$\n630\t\\STATE $\\tau_\\text{mag} \\leftarrow 0.49$, \\quad $\\tau_\\text{vel} \\leftarrow 0.1$, \\quad $\\varepsilon \\leftarrow 10^{-6}$\n631\t\\STATE Initialize $r_\\text{prev} \\leftarrow 0$, $\\Delta r_\\text{prev} \\leftarrow 0$, $\\mathcal{H} \\leftarrow []$\n632\t\\FOR{each checkpoint $t$}\n633\t  \\STATE $r_t \\leftarrow \\bar{V}_t \\,/\\, \\max(\\bar{E}_t,\\, \\varepsilon)$\n634\t  \\STATE $\\Delta r_t \\leftarrow r_t - r_\\text{prev}$\n635\t  \\STATE $c_\\text{mag} \\leftarrow (r_t \\geq \\tau_\\text{mag})$\n636\t  \\STATE $c_\\text{vel} \\leftarrow (\\Delta r_t > \\tau_\\text{vel})$\n637\t  \\STATE $c_\\text{acc} \\leftarrow (\\Delta r_\\text{prev} > \\tau_\\text{vel}) \\wedge (\\Delta r_t > \\Delta r_\\text{prev})$\n638\t  \\STATE $c_\\text{dec} \\leftarrow \\bigl|\\{i : \\mathcal{H}[i] < \\mathcal{H}[i{-}1],\\; i \\in \\text{last 3}\\}\\bigr| \\geq 2$\n639\t  \\IF{$c_\\text{mag} \\wedge c_\\text{vel} \\wedge c_\\text{acc} \\wedge c_\\text{dec}$}\n640\t    \\STATE \\textbf{Alert:} CIVO onset at step $t$\n641\t  \\ENDIF\n642\t  \\STATE $r_\\text{prev} \\leftarrow r_t$, \\quad $\\Delta r_\\text{prev} \\leftarrow \\Delta r_t$\n643\t  \\STATE Append $\\bar{E}_t$ to $\\mathcal{H}$\n644\t\\ENDFOR\n645\t\\end{algorithmic}\n646\t\\end{algorithm}\n647\t\n648\tThe four conditions capture complementary signals: (1)~\\emph{Magnitude} ($r_t \\geq 0.49$) confirms that the value-to-evaluation ratio has reached a level indicative of overestimation; (2)~\\emph{Velocity} ($\\Delta r_t > 0.1$) rules out slow drift or plateau behavior; (3)~\\emph{Acceleration} ($\\Delta r_t > \\Delta r_{t-1}$, both $> 0.1$) filters oscillatory paths; (4)~\\emph{Evaluation decline} ($\\geq 2$ of 3 recent checkpoints decline) confirms that overestimation coincides with actual performance degradation. A full comparison of window sizes appears in \\S\\ref{sec:appendix_sensitivity}.\n649\t\n650\tSensitivity analysis over $\\tau_\\text{mag} \\in [0.30, 0.70]$ confirms that $\\tau_\\text{mag} = 0.49$ maximizes Youden's $J = 0.714$; raising the threshold to $0.50$ loses one true positive (seed~3 triggers at $r_t = 0.497$) with no reduction in false positives. The velocity threshold $\\tau_\\text{vel} = 0.1$ is stable across $[0.05, 0.18]$ (TPR remains $5/5$).\n651\t\n652\t\\subsection{Validation Results}\n653\t\n654\t\\begin{table}[ht]\n655\t\\centering\n656\t\\caption{Retrospective CIVO detector validation ($n{=}14$). Detection type: ``predictive'' = alert precedes 20\\% eval decline; ``reactive'' = alert follows decline onset.}\n657\t\\label{tab:detector-validation}\n658\t\\footnotesize\n659\t\\setlength{\\tabcolsep}{3pt}\n660\t\\begin{tabular}{llccccc}\n661\t\\toprule\n662\t\\textbf{Exp} & \\textbf{Config} & \\textbf{Verdict} & \\textbf{Trigger} & \\textbf{Type} & \\textbf{Peak Eval} & $r_\\text{ve}$ \\\\\n663\t\\midrule\n664\t008d s0 & trained $\\dt$-emb & TP & 45K & predict. & 167 & 0.69 \\\\\n665\t010b & trained $\\dt$-emb (Cheetah) & TP & 65K & reactive & 322 & 3.18 \\\\\n666\t008d s3 & trained $\\dt$-emb & TP & 45K & predict. & 452 & 0.50 \\\\\n667\t008d s4 & trained $\\dt$-emb & TP & 105K & reactive & 383 & 3.03 \\\\\n668\t010b s0 & trained $\\dt$-emb (Cheetah) & TP & 95K & reactive & 248 & 6.34 \\\\\n669\t\\midrule\n670\t008f s3 & no $\\dt$-emb & TN & -- & -- & 594 & -- \\\\\n671\t009b s2 & trained $\\dt$-emb (self-corr.) & TN & -- & -- & 279 & -- \\\\\n672\tbaseline s1 & vanilla & TN & -- & -- & 970 & -- \\\\\n673\t009d s0 & vanilla & TN & -- & -- & 951 & -- \\\\\n674\t009d s1 & vanilla & TN & -- & -- & 920 & -- \\\\\n675\t019b & trained $\\dt$-emb (PickCube) & TN & -- & -- & 26.6 & -- \\\\\n676\t\\midrule\n677\t009c s2 & no $\\dt$-emb & FP & 105K & reactive & 359 & 0.75 \\\\\n678\t009a s2 & vanilla & FP & 85K & predict. & 967 & 0.73 \\\\\n679\t\\midrule\n680\t009c s3 & no $\\dt$-emb & TN & -- & -- & 457 & -- \\\\\n681\t\\midrule\n682\t\\multicolumn{7}{l}{TPR = 5/5 (1.00), \\quad FPR = 2/9 (0.22)} \\\\\n683\t\\bottomrule\n684\t\\end{tabular}\n685\t\\end{table}\n686\t\n687\tThe detector identifies all five CIVO-positive runs (TPR = 5/5). Two are detected predictively (008d seeds~0 and~3, both at 45K). Two false positives occur among nine negative cases (FPR = 2/9, precision 71\\%, F$_1$ = 0.83). Run 009c develops genuine late-stage value overestimation unrelated to CIVO; run 009a triggers during a transient eval dip at 85K before recovery to 966. Early alerts (before 70K) carry higher diagnostic confidence.\n688\t\n689\t\\subsection{Cross-Simulator Specificity (PickCube)}\n690\t\n691\tExp\\_019b applies the CIVO-trigger configuration to PickCube-v1 in ManiSkill3, a manipulation task with sparse rewards. The V/E ratio exhibits an oscillatory pattern rather than monotonic divergence: it spikes to $r_t = 5.66$ at 52.5K, then deflates to 1.38 by 72.5K. This trajectory prevents the acceleration condition from co-occurring with evaluation decline at any single checkpoint. Across all 14 checkpoints through 132.5K, the detector correctly remains silent (TN), confirming that the acceleration filter distinguishes transient spikes from CIVO's sustained monotonic divergence.\n692\t\n693\t\\subsection{Detector Limitations}\n694\t\n695\tFour constraints bound the current detector. First, the three-checkpoint window cannot distinguish transient dips from sustained collapse. Second, the acceleration condition assumes monotonically increasing overestimation during onset. Third, the detector identifies a \\emph{pattern} rather than a \\emph{mechanism}: run 009c shows that non-CIVO pathologies produce the same signature. Fourth, threshold values were calibrated on fourteen runs across three environments; generalization to different reward scales requires further validation.\n696\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01XEbpCEHgN2TEZ2rDrQ5KQK","session_id":"937facd1-3ff3-4809-b45a-58cd38966ef5","uuid":"ae9079d5-18f0-45fc-ad28-4ec5d9998b06","timestamp":"2026-05-20T17:09:18.008Z"}
